#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""session-continuity -- keep an orchestrator's session running, written down and re-grounded.

THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES. An orchestrator is a registration on the bus and a
workpaper on disk. Its process is not part of it. A background session ends, the
desktop app moves a conversation, and the handle reads gone while the work it
names is still open. Mail then sits in that inbox until somebody looks. The wake
rail beside this program tells a live session it has mail and refuses a gone one,
by its own law. This program is the other half: it starts the recorded session
again, under its owner, from its own transcript, and ends it when it goes quiet.

A SESSION THAT IS HERE LOSES STATE TOO, WHICH IS THE SECOND DEFECT. What a
session holds and never wrote down goes when the harness compacts it. Measured
2026-09-26 over twelve scratch sessions: a compaction loses no rule text at all,
and our own four clean checkpoints each added about ten posture clauses the
workpaper did not hold. So the loss is the write that never happened, not the
summary. This program forces that write from outside on a cadence and at a token
threshold, and after a compaction it sends the turn that re-invokes the skill the
run is governed by.

WHAT IT DELIVERS. Two fixed sentences, RESUME_TURN and FORCED_WRITE_TURN below,
each carrying no interpolation at all. One re-invokes the orchestrate skill's
resume operation, and the skill supplies it. The other orders the postures write. Everything the session
then does comes from the bus, from the skill and from its own workpaper. `--law`
re-derives the field set from each string at runtime and the program refuses to
run when either carries a field, so the claim is a command anybody can run rather
than a grep of the author's honesty.

WHY A TURN AND NOT A HOOK LINE. Measured 2026-09-26: a hook's printed sentence
reaches the model's context and the turn's own prompt wins over it. A delivered
slash command expands into the skill when a stopped session is resumed with the
turn as its prompt. The same text written into a live session's socket arrives
as words, and the model then decides whether to invoke anything. One trial of
four answered such an order from memory with no invocation at all, which is the
failure this rail exists to stop. So a live session takes the turn with a hook on
its own side: the orchestrate skill's resume-hook matches RESUME_TURN exactly and
puts the skill's front page in front of the model before its first token. Q184.

A RESUME IS NOT A FORK, AND ONE RESUME FORM MAKES IT ONE. `--resume` continues
one session id and one transcript. A session the harness has already run in the
background carries its own saved options, and flags passed to a resume of that
session start a COPY under a new session id; it says so on stderr, after the copy
exists. Measured 2026-09-25: a first resume with flags continued session
0be4ddbb, the next resume of it with the same flags started 3273f5c5 carrying a
copy of its whole history, and a resume with no flags continued 3273f5c5 and kept
its id.

So there are two forms. A session the harness lists with kind `background` is
resumed bare and wakes with the name, model and permission flag it already has. A
session with no row takes the flags, which set those three the first time. A
listing that cannot be read is refused, because a flagged resume of the wrong
case is the one outcome nothing later can undo.

THE APP'S CONVERSATION WINS. A copy fed by this rail runs turns the app never
shows. So at the moment it would resume a handle's session, on the mail road and
on the gone compaction road, the rail reads the conversation's family: every
transcript that starts with the same first line. When any member's first line
was written by the desktop app, the recorded session is resumed only if it holds
an app line of its own, or has no family. Otherwise the row reads `refused`, and
the app's conversation re-registering the handle ends it. A conversation born on
the command line resumes as before. `--check` shows the reading for every row.

THE RAIL NEVER RESUMES A STALE ID. The app moves a conversation to a new session
id, and the handle records the old one until the new session registers again.
So before a resume of the app's conversation, the rail follows the moves from
the recorded session to the newest. A move sits in the same projects folder,
starts with the old session's last title, and carries message ids copied from
it. A newer session that is live gets the nudge; one that is not is resumed in
the recorded session's place, and it registers the handle. A link the rail
cannot read cleanly refuses the row and names the case: no title, the title in
another folder, a fork, an overlap, or a move the app did not write. `--check`
shows the reading as `rule_c` on every row of the app's conversations.

THE TWIN CHECK, BOTH HALVES, BEFORE ANY RESUME.

  1. No live process on this machine carries the handle's session id. Read from
     the harness's own session records through `session-guard`, which is the one
     reading of that question the wake rail and the sweeper also take.
  2. No live session carries the handle's recorded address or its handle name.
     The app can continue a conversation under a new session id while the bus
     still records the old one, and the name is what survives that move.

Either half true means the session is already here. The handle is handed to the
wake rail's adapter for a nudge and nothing is resumed.

THE RAIL ENDS WHAT IT STARTED. A resumed session does not exit by itself.
Measured 2026-09-25: one sat idle 29 minutes holding 351 MB and was still there.
So a pass stops a session this rail resumed once the harness listing reads it
idle and its transcript has not grown for the threshold. Two readings, because
the listing alone would end a session between two turns of work. `claude stop`
keeps the conversation, so the next pass resumes the same session from the same
transcript. Only what this rail resumed, named in its state file, and only once.

WHAT IT REFUSES, RATHER THAN WARNS ABOUT. A handle whose record carries no
session id: there is nothing to resume, and searching for one is three chances to
resume a stranger. A recorded working directory this account cannot enter: a
session started somewhere else is a session in the wrong place. A bus directory
other than the one /etc/orchestrate.conf names: this program starts processes,
and a bus nobody declared is not the board it may act on.

A FAILED RESUME WRITES A HOLD. A resume that exits non-zero, or whose process is
not alive thirty seconds later, writes a hold carrying the exit code and the last
line of stderr. The hold's scope comes from the sign-in reading.

  account  the sign-in reading is `signed-out`. No handle of this account is
           resumed until `--clear-hold` clears it. A harness signed out refuses
           every resume, and a rail that kept trying would start a login attempt
           every two minutes.
  handle   any other failure. Only that handle is held, and every other handle
           goes on being resumed. Measured 2026-09-30: one folder the harness
           did not trust stopped every orchestrator of an account for 23 hours,
           with the account signed in. A handle's hold is tried again once
           HOLD_RETRY_SECS has passed. A resume that succeeds clears it.

The sign-in reading is `commonclaw-signin-check --read` beside this program,
which is the harness's own answer and the reading the hourly sign-in alarm
takes. A reading of a credentials file would not do: a file can be present for a
login the server refused long ago. `kind` reads `sign-out` or `resume-failed`,
and `scope` reads `account` or `handle`. `--check` shows both for every hold.

A HOLD SENDS ONE LINE, TO TWO READERS AT MOST. The line goes to the `human`
handle, and to the handle ORCHESTRATE_HOLD_NOTIFY names in the conf, when it
names one. It names the held handle, the harness's refusal in its own words, and
the one step that fixes it. A handle's hold that fails again for the same reason
sends nothing new.

A PERSON HEARS OF A HANDLE'S HOLD FROM ROOT, NOT FROM HERE. Nobody reads
`human`. The hold check, commonclaw-hold-check.sh under a root timer, reads each
account's holds from this program's state files and sends one line per hold to
the claw's alert channel and one mail to HOLD_ALERT_TO. It runs as root because
the channel resolves only under the machine's credential, which this account
cannot read. So a hold records three fields for it: `id`, new for each hold and
kept by every retry, `class`, `untrusted` or `refused`, and `cwd`, the handle's
folder. The hold check reads those and never the harness's words. An account
hold sends nothing there, because the hourly sign-in alarm already says the same
thing to the same people.

Nudging and stopping go on under a hold. Neither is the act that was failing.

THE FORCED WRITE, ON A CADENCE AND AT A TOKEN THRESHOLD. A live orchestrator gets
FORCED_WRITE_TURN through the wake rail's adapter when either arm reads due.

  the cadence arm  two hours of the handle's own activity since the last forced
                   write. Activity is the transcript growing, not the clock: a
                   session nobody is using accrues none, and a rail that was off
                   for a day credits none of it. Each pass adds the gap since the
                   last reading, and no more than ACTIVITY_CREDIT_CAP_SECS of it,
                   which is why an off rail cannot bank hours.
  the token arm    the transcript's last turn reads past 90% of the automatic
                   compaction window: the one a person set, or else the
                   harness's default for the session's model, and off for a
                   model with no known default. It fires once per crossing: the arm re-arms
                   only after a reading below the threshold, which is what a
                   compaction produces. Without that, every pass past the
                   threshold would fire.

THE STAMP IS THE WORKPAPER'S MODIFICATION TIME, AND IT IS A WEAK READING. The
write is a model act and this program cannot do it, so the only thing it can read
is whether the file moved. A workpaper that has not moved thirty minutes after
the turn went out is one line to the `human` handle, once per nudge. A workpaper
that moved for some other reason, a journal line say, reads as a write that
happened. That is the honest limit of reading a file's clock, and the arm still
catches the case it was built for: nothing at all happened.

FINDING THE WORKPAPER. The handle's record carries the directory the session
started in, and nothing on the bus carries the workpaper's path. So the rail
resolves it in three steps: a path recorded in the handle's own state file; then
`workpaper.md` in the recorded directory; then the one `workpaper.md` at most
three levels under it, skipping dot-directories and `zz-archive`. Zero or several
means unresolved, and unresolved sends the turn anyway, because the session knows
its own workpaper and the write is what matters. The stamp check is then skipped
and one line names the handle and the candidates.

AFTER A COMPACTION, TWO ROADS. A hook beside this program records one line per
compaction under this account's state directory. A pass that finds a record for
an orchestrator handle picks the road by whether the session is here:

  live  RESUME_TURN goes into the session through the wake rail's adapter, and
        the resume hook on the receiving side loads the skill. Nothing is
        stopped, so a desktop session with no job id is reached like any other.
  gone  the session is resumed with RESUME_TURN as its prompt, bare or flagged by
        the same rule as the mail road. A resume aimed at a live session forks,
        which is why this road is only for a session that is gone.

Both roads are refused while the phrase cannot be read from the skill, and the
record is kept for the pass that can.

The record is consumed by the act. Thirty minutes later the stamp check reads the
workpaper, because the resume operation records itself there, and a workpaper
that did not move is one line to the `human` handle.

AN ACTING PASS PRUNES ITS OWN STATE DIRECTORY. A handle's file that nothing has
written to in thirty days is removed, unless it carries a hold or names a session
this rail resumed and has not stopped. Both of those say something is outstanding,
and outstanding does not expire. A compaction record older than
RECORD_TTL_HOURS goes the same way.

  session-continuity --check           print what a pass would do; do nothing
  session-continuity --once            one pass; this is what the timer runs
  session-continuity --once <handle>   the same pass, acting on those handles
                                       alone. What the board says is unchanged:
                                       every other handle is still listed with
                                       the action it would have taken. It is
                                       what brings one orchestrator back without
                                       waking every one an account holds
  session-continuity --law             print both fixed sentences and prove
                                       neither carries interpolation
  session-continuity --clear-hold <h>  clear that handle's hold, of either scope

  --idle-stop-minutes <n>              how long a session this rail resumed may
                                       sit quiet before a pass ends it. It is
                                       there to drive a proof; the units pass
                                       none and take the default
  --activity-hours <n>                 the cadence arm's threshold, in hours of
                                       activity. Same reason: the units pass none
  --set-workpaper <handle> <path>      record which workpaper a handle's stamp is
                                       read from, where the search cannot say
  --allow-source-tree                  let a copy that is not the installed one
                                       act. Without it, such a copy's --once
                                       prints what it would do and acts on
                                       nothing, because it would reach the
                                       claw's real bus through the installed
                                       wake rail

EXIT CODES. 0 the pass ran. 1 a sentence law is broken. 2 usage. 3 a wall: the
bus is not the declared one, session-guard is missing, the wake rail this program
hands a twin to is missing, or the harness command line is absent. 5 this copy
is not the installed one and --allow-source-tree was not passed, so the pass
acted on nothing.
"""

import datetime
import importlib.machinery
import importlib.util
import json
import os
import pwd
import re
import shutil
import string
import subprocess
import sys
import time
import uuid

# ------------------------------------------------------------ THE SENTENCES
#
# NO format field, and no other way to build delivered text. The wake rail
# beside this program allows itself one field, the bus directory, because its
# sentence names the bus to read. Neither of these names anything: the session
# each reaches finds its own bus the way it found it before.
#
# RESUME_TURN IS THE ORCHESTRATE SKILL'S PHRASE, AND THIS PROGRAM READS IT FROM
# THE SKILL. Q191, ruled 2026-09-26: the skill owns the resume hook, and the
# phrase is the seam between the hook and this rail. `resume-hook --phrase`
# prints it. It is read once at start, with one trailing newline taken off, and
# this file keeps no copy. A copy would drift from what the hook matches, and
# nothing would say so.
#
# THE PHRASE IS A SLASH COMMAND ON PURPOSE. Measured 2026-09-26: a turn a stopped
# session is resumed with expands into the skill, with the body in front of the
# model before its first token. A sentence in prose, on that same road, was
# answered with "What would you like me to do?". So the words are the invocation
# and nothing else.
#
# WITHOUT THE SKILL THERE IS NO PHRASE, AND BOTH RESUME ROADS REFUSE. The wake
# nudge and the forced write do not need it and go on.
RESUME_HOOK = "/etc/claude-code/.claude/skills/orchestrate/scripts/resume-hook"
RESUME_TURN = None
RESUME_TURN_WHY = "the resume phrase has not been read"

# FORCED_WRITE_TURN NAMES BOTH HALVES OF THE WRITE. The postures are what a
# compaction drops; the skills in use are what a resume has to re-invoke. A turn
# that asked only for postures would leave the resume guessing which skill it is
# running under, which is the failure the ruling of 2026-09-26 names.
FORCED_WRITE_TURN = (
    "checkpoint now: write your standing postures and your skills in use to the "
    "workpaper, then continue."
)



def sentences():
    """Every fixed sentence this program can deliver now. `--law` proves each one.

    The resume phrase is in the list once it has been read from the skill.
    """
    out = [("FORCED_WRITE_TURN", FORCED_WRITE_TURN)]
    if RESUME_TURN is not None:
        out.insert(0, ("RESUME_TURN", RESUME_TURN))
    return out


def read_resume_turn():
    """The resume phrase as the skill's hook prints it, or (None, why)."""
    if not os.access(RESUME_HOOK, os.X_OK):
        return None, "no orchestrate skill in the machine tier: there is no %s" % RESUME_HOOK
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run([RESUME_HOOK, "--phrase"], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                              stderr=subprocess.PIPE, timeout=10)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        return None, "%s --phrase did not run: %s: %s" % (RESUME_HOOK, type(exc).__name__, exc)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None, "%s --phrase exited %d" % (RESUME_HOOK, proc.returncode)
    text = proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace")
    if text.endswith("\n"):
        text = text[:-1]
    if not text.strip() or "\n" in text:
        return None, "%s --phrase printed no single line" % RESUME_HOOK
    return text, ""


def load_resume_turn():
    """Read the phrase once, at start, into RESUME_TURN."""
    global RESUME_TURN, RESUME_TURN_WHY
    RESUME_TURN, RESUME_TURN_WHY = read_resume_turn()
    return RESUME_TURN


def no_phrase_detail():
    return "%s, so there is no resume phrase to send" % RESUME_TURN_WHY

# %-style and str.format are the two ways a Python string interpolates. The law
# check below proves neither sentence carries either.
PERCENT_FIELD = re.compile(r"%[^%]")

# What a model id looks like, which is anything the harness accepts after
# --model. The point of the test is what it excludes: the harness's own
# `<synthetic>` placeholder, and an empty field.
MODEL_ID = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._-]*$")

# The bracketed reference the agent listing appends to a name it shows twice. A
# handle records the listing's whole string as its address, and the harness's
# session record carries the name without it, so a comparison of the two has to
# take the reference off first.
LISTING_REF = re.compile(r"\s*\[[0-9a-f]{4,}\]$")

CONF = os.environ.get("ORCHESTRATE_CONF", "/etc/orchestrate.conf")
BIN_DIR = "/opt/commonclaw/bin"
# The one path this program acts from. See installed_copy().
INSTALLED = os.path.join(BIN_DIR, "session-continuity")
HERE = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(__file__))
ROLE = "orchestrator"
# How long a handle's own hold waits before the rail tries that handle again.
# The fix is a person's act, and an hour is soon enough after it. A harness that
# refuses every two minutes would also fill the journal.
HOLD_RETRY_SECS = 3600
# The conf key that names a second reader for a hold's line. Empty or absent
# means the `human` handle alone.
HOLD_NOTIFY_KEY = "ORCHESTRATE_HOLD_NOTIFY"
# What a bus handle looks like. A conf value of another shape is not sent to.
HANDLE_NAME = re.compile(r"^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_.-]*$")
# How long the resumed process gets to appear in the harness's session records.
# A background session writes its record as it starts; thirty seconds is the
# measured start plus room, and it is the whole cost of a pass that resumed.
ALIVE_AFTER_SECS = 30
# A `claude --bg` call returns as soon as the session is started. A call still
# running after this long has not returned at all, which is its own failure.
SPAWN_TIMEOUT_SECS = 120
# How long a session this rail resumed may sit quiet before the rail ends it.
# `--idle-stop-minutes` moves it for a proof; the units pass none.
IDLE_STOP_MINUTES = 15
# The cadence arm: hours of the handle's own activity between forced writes.
# `--activity-hours` moves it for a proof; the units pass none.
ACTIVITY_HOURS = 2
# The most activity one pass may credit. The timer ticks every two minutes, so a
# gap wider than this is the rail having been off, not the session having worked.
ACTIVITY_CREDIT_CAP_SECS = 600
# How long the workpaper has to move after a forced write before the miss is one
# line in the human's queue.
STAMP_GRACE_MINUTES = 30
# How deep under the handle's recorded directory the workpaper search looks.
WORKPAPER_SEARCH_DEPTH = 3
# Directory names the workpaper search never enters. A retired repository holds
# workpapers that are records, and a dot-directory holds worktrees whose copies
# would each answer the search.
WORKPAPER_SKIP = ("zz-archive", "node_modules")
# A compaction record older than this is dropped. It is long enough that a rail
# stopped overnight still acts on the morning's compaction, and short enough that
# a record nothing can act on stops being asked about.
RECORD_TTL_HOURS = 24
# The file the compaction hook appends to, under this account's state directory.
COMPACTION_RECORDS = "compactions.jsonl"
# Where the machine's policy tier sits. The rail reads the automatic compaction
# window from it where a firm set one there; provisioning writes none.
MANAGED_SETTINGS = "/etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json"
# The name the harness reads the window from, and the settings key beside it.
WINDOW_ENV = "CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_COMPACT_WINDOW"
WINDOW_KEY = "autoCompactWindow"
# THE TOKEN ARM FIRES AT THIS FRACTION OF THE WINDOW. Ruled 2026-09-28: 90
# percent, where it was half. The write then comes close to the compaction it
# prepares for. It leaves 65,000 tokens of room at the 650,000 window
# provisioning seeds, and 100,000 at a 1M model's default.
TOKEN_ARM_FRACTION = 0.9
# THE HARNESS'S OWN DEFAULT, PER MODEL, WHEN NOTHING DECLARES A WINDOW. The
# documented context window of each model family, matched on the start of the
# model id the transcript records. Ruled 2026-09-26: 200K for Haiku 4.5 and the
# older models, 1M for Fable and Sonnet 5. A model this table does not name gets
# no token arm at all, because a guessed window fires the arm at the wrong place
# and a missing one only leaves the cadence arm to do the work.
MODEL_WINDOWS = (
    ("claude-fable-5", 1000000),
    ("claude-sonnet-5", 1000000),
    ("claude-haiku-4-5", 200000),
    ("claude-opus-4", 200000),
    ("claude-sonnet-4", 200000),
    ("claude-haiku-4", 200000),
    ("claude-3", 200000),
)


def law_fields(text):
    return {f for _, f, _, _ in string.Formatter().parse(text) if f is not None}


def sentence_law():
    """Every fixed sentence, with the fields it carries. Empty sets mean lawful."""
    out = []
    for name, text in sentences():
        fields = sorted(law_fields(text))
        percent = PERCENT_FIELD.search(text)
        out.append({"name": name, "sentence": text, "format_fields": fields,
                    "percent_fields": percent.group(0) if percent else None,
                    "lawful": not fields and not percent})
    return out


def lawful():
    return all(s["lawful"] for s in sentence_law())


def log(msg):
    sys.stderr.write(msg + "\n")
    sys.stderr.flush()


def now_iso():
    return datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc).replace(microsecond=0).isoformat()


# --------------------------------------------------------------- the settings
#
# The bus and the fallback model come from the orchestrate settings seam's
# machine layer, which is the file every tool here already reads. This program
# adds no conf of its own: one more file would be one more place the bus is
# named, and two names for one bus is the fault this rail is most able to cause.
def conf_value(key):
    pattern = re.compile(r"^[ \t]*%s[ \t]*[:=][ \t]*(.*)$" % re.escape(key))
    try:
        with open(CONF, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                m = pattern.match(line)
                if not m:
                    continue
                raw = m.group(1).split("#", 1)[0].strip()
                if len(raw) >= 2 and raw[0] == raw[-1] and raw[0] in "\"'":
                    raw = raw[1:-1]
                return raw or None
    except OSError:
        return None
    return None


def beside(name):
    """A sibling of this program, or the copy the claw installs.

    The four rails land in one directory on a claw, so a sibling is the ordinary
    answer. A program read out of a source tree has no siblings, and there the
    installed copy is the one that agrees with the running rail.
    """
    local = os.path.join(HERE, name)
    if os.path.exists(local):
        return local
    return os.path.join(BIN_DIR, name)


def installed_copy():
    """Whether this process runs the installed program, by its resolved path."""
    return os.path.realpath(__file__) == os.path.realpath(INSTALLED)


def state_dir():
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_STATE_HOME") or os.path.expanduser("~/.local/state")
    return os.path.join(base, "commonclaw", "continuity")


def harness():
    """The agent command line, or "" when this account has none.

    It is installed per account, under a home directory, so a unit's minimal
    PATH does not find it and the home path is the fallback.
    """
    found = shutil.which("claude")
    if found:
        return found
    guess = os.path.expanduser("~/.local/bin/claude")
    return guess if os.access(guess, os.X_OK) else ""


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- the readings
def load_module(path, name):
    """A program beside this one, loaded as a module, or (None, why).

    Without bytecode: this file runs from a skill tree and from the machine's bin
    directory, and a cache written into either is a file nobody shipped.
    """
    if not os.path.isfile(path):
        return None, "no %s at %s" % (name, path)
    sys.dont_write_bytecode = True
    try:
        loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader(name.replace("-", "_"), path)
        spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader(loader.name, loader)
        mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
        loader.exec_module(mod)
    except Exception as exc:  # a broken sibling is reported, never half-used
        return None, "%s at %s did not load: %s: %s" % (name, path, type(exc).__name__, exc)
    return mod, ""


def unread_count(bus_dir, handle):
    """How many messages sit past the handle's cursor, and the newest one's id."""
    inbox = os.path.join(bus_dir, "inbox", "%s.jsonl" % handle)
    try:
        with open(inbox, "rb") as fh:
            lines = [b for b in fh.read().splitlines() if b.strip()]
    except OSError:
        return 0, None
    cursor = 0
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(bus_dir, "cursors", "%s.cursor" % handle), "r") as fh:
            cursor = int(fh.read().strip() or 0)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        cursor = 0
    if len(lines) <= cursor:
        return 0, None
    newest = None
    try:
        newest = json.loads(lines[-1].decode("utf-8", "replace")).get("id")
    except ValueError:
        newest = None
    return len(lines) - cursor, newest


def saved_sessions(listing):
    """The sessions that carry their own saved options: the background rows.

    None when the listing could not be read, which is not the same answer as an
    empty one. One says nothing is saved, the other says nothing is known.

    A session is saved when any of its rows is a background row. listed_sessions()
    keeps one row per session and marks it `background_row` when another row of
    the same session was the background one.
    """
    if listing is None:
        return None
    return {sid for sid, row in listing.items()
            if row.get("kind") == "background" or row.get("background_row")}


def listed_sessions(cmd):
    """Every session the harness lists, by session id, or None when it cannot be read.

    One subprocess a pass, and two questions come out of it: which sessions carry
    saved options, and which of the ones this rail resumed are idle now.

    ONE SESSION CAN HAVE TWO ROWS, AND THE LAST ONE IS KEPT. The app opening a
    session that has a background job adds an interactive row after the
    background one. Read 2026-09-27 on session 0be4ddbb. Keyed by session id, the
    interactive row hid the background row. When the app's process ended between
    the listing and the twin check, the rail would take the flagged form for a session
    with saved options, and that form starts a copy. So the background rows are
    read over all rows first, and the kept row carries `background_row`.
    """
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run([cmd, "agents", "--json", "--all"], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                              stderr=subprocess.PIPE, timeout=60)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return None
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return None
    try:
        data = json.loads(proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace") or "null")
    except ValueError:
        return None
    rows = data if isinstance(data, list) else (data or {}).get("agents")
    if not isinstance(rows, list):
        return None
    rows = [r for r in rows
            if isinstance(r, dict) and isinstance(r.get("sessionId"), str) and r["sessionId"]]
    background = {r["sessionId"] for r in rows if r.get("kind") == "background"}
    out = {r["sessionId"]: r for r in rows}
    for sid in background:
        out[sid]["background_row"] = True
    return out


def transcript_path(session):
    """The transcript file for one session id, or "".

    The project directory is named for the directory a session started in, which
    is not always the one the handle recorded, so the file is found by its own
    name. The session id is unique, so the search cannot land on a stranger.
    """
    root = os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/projects")
    try:
        for project in sorted(os.listdir(root)):
            candidate = os.path.join(root, project, "%s.jsonl" % session)
            if os.path.isfile(candidate):
                return candidate
    except OSError:
        return ""
    return ""


# ------------------------------------------------------- the app's conversation
#
# RULE A: THE RAIL FOLLOWS THE CLIENT THAT STARTED THE CONVERSATION. Q196, ruled
# 2026-09-27. A conversation the desktop app shows is resumed only under the
# session id the app writes into. A conversation born on the command line is
# resumed as before, so a command-line orchestrator is never cut off.
APP_ENTRYPOINT = "claude-desktop"


def transcript_lines(path, first_only=False):
    """(uuid, entrypoint) for each line that carries a uuid. [] when unreadable."""
    out = []
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                if '"uuid"' not in line:
                    continue
                try:
                    rec = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                if not (isinstance(rec, dict) and rec.get("uuid")):
                    continue
                out.append((str(rec["uuid"]), str(rec.get("entrypoint") or "")))
                if first_only:
                    break
    except OSError:
        return []
    return out


def conversation_reading(session):
    """Rule A's reading of one recorded session: resume or refused, and why.

    THE FAMILY. Every transcript under the account's projects folder whose first
    uuid line carries the recorded session's first uuid. The app's move to a new
    id and the command line's copy both start with the original's first line.

    THE ORIGIN IS READ FROM THE WHOLE FAMILY. A copy made by the command line
    rewrites `entrypoint` on every line it copies. Measured 2026-09-27: session
    3273f5c5 read `cli` on all 310 lines it shared with 0be4ddbb, where 0be4ddbb
    read `claude-desktop`. The command line never writes the app's entrypoint, so
    the app's entrypoint on any member's first line says the app holds this
    conversation. Otherwise the recorded transcript's own first entrypoint
    answers.

    THE APP'S CONVERSATION WINS. With the app's origin, the recorded session is
    resumed only when it holds a line from the app whose uuid no other member
    carries, or when it has no family. Any other origin, or none recorded,
    resumes as before.

    It reads every transcript's first uuid line and the family's members whole,
    so it runs only where a resume is being decided.
    """
    reading = {"origin": "", "family": [], "own_app_lines": 0,
               "verdict": "resume", "reason": ""}
    path = transcript_path(session)
    if not path:
        reading["reason"] = "no transcript for the session, so nothing records its origin"
        return reading
    lines = transcript_lines(path)
    if not lines:
        reading["reason"] = "the transcript carries no line with a uuid"
        return reading
    first, first_entry = lines[0]
    root = os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/projects")
    family = []
    try:
        projects = sorted(os.listdir(root))
    except OSError:
        projects = []
    for project in projects:
        folder = os.path.join(root, project)
        try:
            names = sorted(os.listdir(folder))
        except OSError:
            continue
        for name in names:
            candidate = os.path.join(folder, name)
            if not name.endswith(".jsonl") or os.path.realpath(candidate) == os.path.realpath(path):
                continue
            head = transcript_lines(candidate, first_only=True)
            if head and head[0][0] == first:
                family.append((name[:-len(".jsonl")], candidate, head[0][1]))
    reading["family"] = [sid for sid, _, _ in family]
    origin = first_entry or next((e for _, e in lines if e), "")
    if first_entry == APP_ENTRYPOINT or any(e == APP_ENTRYPOINT for _, _, e in family):
        origin = APP_ENTRYPOINT
    reading["origin"] = origin or "none recorded"
    if origin != APP_ENTRYPOINT:
        reading["reason"] = "the conversation began on %s, so it resumes as before" % (
            origin or "a client that recorded no entrypoint")
        return reading
    if not family:
        reading["reason"] = "the app's conversation, with no other session sharing its start"
        return reading
    others = set()
    for _, candidate, _ in family:
        others.update(u for u, _ in transcript_lines(candidate))
    own = sum(1 for u, e in lines if e == APP_ENTRYPOINT and u not in others)
    reading["own_app_lines"] = own
    if own:
        reading["reason"] = ("the app's conversation: %d app lines are this session's own" % own)
        return reading
    reading["verdict"] = "refused"
    reading["reason"] = (
        "session %s records a copy of a conversation the desktop app shows: it holds no "
        "line the app wrote that %s does not also carry. The refusal ends when the app's "
        "conversation re-registers the handle"
        % (session[:8], ", ".join(sid[:8] for sid in reading["family"])))
    return reading


# ------------------------------------------------- the app's moved conversation
#
# RULE C: THE RAIL NEVER RESUMES A STALE ID. The app moves a conversation to a
# new session id, and the handle records the old one until the new session
# registers again. A resume of the old id starts a session the person cannot see,
# and its resume operation then registers the handle to itself. So before it
# resumes, the rail finds the newest session of the conversation the handle
# records, and resumes that one, or nudges it when it is live.
#
# THE LINK IS THE MESSAGE IDS A MOVE COPIES, MEASURED 2026-09-27 ON THE HUB
# (w314 piece 1, w319 piece 1). The moved session sits in the same projects
# folder, the app wrote its first line, it began after the old session's last
# line, and it opens with lines copied from the old session, message ids and
# all. A message id is random and written once, so two sessions share one only
# when one copied the other. So one shared id is the floor, and no higher count
# is asked for. The 40 moves the rule links on the hub copied 138 to 4381 ids,
# and a tenant claw's two copied 185 and 1483. Two conversations in one folder that share
# no id never link, whatever their titles: one folder held two conversations
# under one title 9 times.
#
# THE TITLE IS A FURTHER CHECK, WHERE ONE EXISTS. The app does not title a
# conversation by itself. It wrote no title on 51 of its 98 sessions on the hub,
# and on none of its 4 on a tenant claw. Of the hub's 47 titled sessions, 29 got the title
# from a move and 16 had it set in the app. 2 got it from a resume that passed
# the handle's name with --name, as the rail's flagged resume does (w315 drove
# it). Once a title exists, every move starts with it: 26 of 26 on the hub. So
# where the session a move left carries a title, the move must start with it,
# or the rail refuses. On the hub the walk with the title and the walk on ids
# alone read the same on all 47 titled sessions. The title never links without
# ids.
#
# THE LINK RESTS ON THE DESKTOP APP, AND NOBODY HERE CONTROLS IT. A version of
# the app that moves a conversation and copies no id makes the move invisible to
# this rule, and the rail goes back to resuming the old id. Re-measure it with
# `python3 _workpapers/w314/measure-moves.py <bus dir>` from the source root.
# Under "1. pairs the clock calls a move" it is clean when:
#   - for titled pairs, the count for "carries message ids" equals the count for
#     "title is its line 0" (23 of 24 each on the hub);
#   - for untitled pairs, the count for "opens with one block copied from the
#     first" equals the count "sharing message ids" (27 of 27 on the hub, 2 of 2
#     on that claw).
# A fall in "sharing message ids" against the clock's own count is the sign the
# app has stopped copying them: 27 of 34 on the hub, 2 of 2 on that claw.
TITLE_TYPE = "custom-title"


# ONE READING OF A TRANSCRIPT A PROCESS. `--check` reads rule C for every row,
# and each row reads every transcript that changed after its session began.
# Read afresh per row, that took 41 seconds over twelve handles on the hub. So a
# reading is kept for the life of the process, keyed by the file's size and
# modification time, and a file that changed is read again.
FACTS = {}


def transcript_facts(path, whole=True):
    """What rule C reads from one transcript, or None when it cannot be read.

    The titles in order, and the first line time. With `whole`, also the last
    line time, the entrypoint of the first line that carries a uuid, and every
    message id. A transcript in another projects folder is read for its titles
    alone, and only its title lines and its first timed line are parsed.
    """
    try:
        st = os.stat(path)
    except OSError:
        return None
    key = (path, st.st_size, st.st_mtime)
    for want in ((True, False) if not whole else (True,)):
        if (key, want) in FACTS:
            return FACTS[(key, want)]
    facts = {"titles": [], "first_at": "", "last_at": "", "entry": "", "uuids": set()}
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                title_line = TITLE_TYPE in line
                if not (whole or title_line or not facts["first_at"]):
                    continue
                try:
                    rec = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                if not isinstance(rec, dict):
                    continue
                if title_line and rec.get("type") == TITLE_TYPE \
                        and isinstance(rec.get("customTitle"), str):
                    facts["titles"].append(rec["customTitle"])
                stamp = rec.get("timestamp")
                if isinstance(stamp, str) and stamp:
                    facts["first_at"] = facts["first_at"] or stamp
                    facts["last_at"] = stamp
                if whole and rec.get("uuid"):
                    if not facts["uuids"]:
                        facts["entry"] = str(rec.get("entrypoint") or "")
                    facts["uuids"].add(str(rec["uuid"]))
    except OSError:
        return None
    FACTS[(key, whole)] = facts
    return facts


def stamp_epoch(stamp):
    try:
        return datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(stamp.replace("Z", "+00:00")).timestamp()
    except (AttributeError, ValueError):
        return None


def moved_reading(session):
    """Rule C's reading of one recorded session: which session of its conversation is newest.

    `verdict` is `resume` when the recorded session is the newest, `newer
    session` when a move follows it, and `refused` when the link is ambiguous.
    `case` names what made it ambiguous. `path` is every session from the
    recorded one to the newest, and `pairing` says what linked each move.

    It reads every transcript that changed after the recorded session began, so
    it runs only where a resume is being decided.
    """
    reading = {"recorded": session, "newest": session, "path": [session], "pairing": [],
               "verdict": "resume", "case": "", "reason": ""}
    path = transcript_path(session)
    if not path:
        reading["reason"] = "no transcript for the session, so nothing records a move"
        return reading
    mine = transcript_facts(path)
    if mine is None or not mine["uuids"]:
        reading["reason"] = "the transcript carries no line with a uuid"
        return reading
    folder = os.path.dirname(path)
    since = stamp_epoch(mine["first_at"])
    root = os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/projects")
    later = {}
    try:
        projects = sorted(os.listdir(root))
    except OSError:
        projects = []
    for project in projects:
        where = os.path.join(root, project)
        try:
            names = sorted(os.listdir(where))
        except OSError:
            continue
        for name in names:
            candidate = os.path.join(where, name)
            if not name.endswith(".jsonl") or os.path.realpath(candidate) == os.path.realpath(path):
                continue
            try:
                if since is not None and os.path.getmtime(candidate) < since:
                    continue
            except OSError:
                continue
            facts = transcript_facts(candidate, whole=where == folder)
            if facts and facts["first_at"] and facts["first_at"] > mine["first_at"]:
                later[name[:-len(".jsonl")]] = dict(facts, folder=where)
    here = {sid: f for sid, f in later.items() if f["folder"] == folder}

    def refuse(case, why):
        reading["verdict"], reading["case"] = "refused", case
        reading["reason"] = ("rule C, %s: %s. The rail resumes nothing for this handle until the "
                             "app's conversation re-registers it" % (case, why))
        return reading

    title = mine["titles"][-1] if mine["titles"] else ""
    elsewhere = sorted(sid for sid, f in later.items()
                       if title and f["folder"] != folder and title in f["titles"])
    if elsewhere:
        return refuse("title in another folder", "%s in another projects folder carries the "
                      "title '%s'" % (", ".join(s[:8] for s in elsewhere), title))
    # ONE MOVE AT A TIME, AND EACH MOVE CARRIES LINES OF THE ONE BEFORE IT. A move
    # copies part of the session it left, so after the first step a move must hold
    # a line the step before did not already carry. Two sessions that each copied
    # the recorded one, and not each other, are then a fork, not a line of moves.
    # The first step takes any shared id, so a short branch whose own lines no
    # later session carries steps to its sibling. That is one history (w319).
    # The copied ids link a move. A title, where the session it left carries one,
    # must be the move's first title, or the link refuses.
    current, facts, linked, before = session, mine, set(), set()
    while True:
        last = facts["titles"][-1] if facts["titles"] else ""
        moves = sorted((sid for sid, f in here.items()
                        if sid not in reading["path"] and f["first_at"] > facts["first_at"]
                        and (f["uuids"] & facts["uuids"]) - before),
                       key=lambda sid: here[sid]["first_at"])
        linked.update(moves)
        if not moves:
            break
        nxt = here[moves[0]]
        if nxt["entry"] != APP_ENTRYPOINT:
            return refuse("not the app's", "session %s links to %s by folder and message ids, and "
                          "its first line was written by %s" % (
                              moves[0][:8], current[:8], nxt["entry"] or "a client that recorded none"))
        if facts["last_at"] > nxt["first_at"]:
            return refuse("overlap", "session %s wrote at %s, after the move to %s began at %s" % (
                current[:8], facts["last_at"], moves[0][:8], nxt["first_at"]))
        if last and nxt["titles"][:1] != [last]:
            return refuse("title differs", "session %s carries the title '%s', and %s, which "
                          "carries its lines, %s" % (
                              current[:8], last, moves[0][:8],
                              "starts with another title" if nxt["titles"] else "carries none"))
        reading["pairing"].append({
            "from": current, "to": moves[0], "link": "title and ids" if last else "ids",
            "title": last, "folder": os.path.basename(folder),
            "shared_ids": len(nxt["uuids"] & facts["uuids"])})
        reading["path"].append(moves[0])
        before = facts["uuids"]
        current, facts = moves[0], nxt
    stray = sorted(linked - set(reading["path"]))
    if stray:
        return refuse("fork", "%s and %s both link to the conversation and are not one line of "
                      "moves" % (", ".join(s[:8] for s in stray), reading["path"][-1][:8]))
    if current == session:
        reading["reason"] = ("%sno later session in its folder carries its lines, so nothing "
                             "moved it" % ("" if title else "no title, and "))
        return reading
    reading["newest"], reading["verdict"] = current, "newer session"
    links = {p["link"] for p in reading["pairing"]}
    where = os.path.basename(folder)
    reading["reason"] = "the app moved the conversation: %s, each move linked by %s" % (
        " to ".join(s[:8] for s in reading["path"]),
        "title '%s' in folder %s and copied message ids" % (title, where)
        if links == {"title and ids"} else
        "copied message ids in folder %s" % where if links == {"ids"} else
        "copied message ids in folder %s, and by the title where the session it left carries "
        "one" % where)
    return reading


def transcript_model(session):
    """The model the transcript's last assistant turn records, or "".

    A resumed session that came back on another model is a charter running
    against dispositions it was not written for. The transcript is the only
    record of which one it was, and its path is not derivable from the handle:
    the project directory is named for the directory the session started in,
    which is not always the one the handle recorded. So the file is found by its
    own name, which is the session id and is unique.

    A TURN THE HARNESS WROTE FOR ITSELF NAMES NO MODEL. It records the word
    `<synthetic>` in the model field, and a resume asking for that exits with a
    usage error. Read on this machine 2026-09-25: four of six resumable
    orchestrator transcripts ended on such a turn. So a value not shaped like a
    model id is passed over, and the newest one that is shaped like one answers.
    """
    path = transcript_path(session)
    if not path:
        return "", ""
    model = ""
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line:
                    continue
                try:
                    rec = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                msg = rec.get("message") if isinstance(rec, dict) else None
                if not (isinstance(msg, dict) and msg.get("role") == "assistant"):
                    continue
                if MODEL_ID.match(str(msg.get("model") or "")):
                    model = str(msg["model"])
    except OSError:
        return "", path
    return model, path


def transcript_tokens(path):
    """The window the transcript's last assistant turn was sent with, or None.

    The two cache fields are the window as sent: what was read out of the cache
    plus what this turn wrote into it. A turn carrying neither says nothing, and
    the newest turn that carries them answers. No session can read its own
    fullness, so this file is the only place the number exists.

    A TURN NO MODEL SENT SAYS NOTHING EITHER. The harness writes its own
    assistant turns under the model `<synthetic>`, with both fields at zero.
    Read as a window, one of them re-armed the token arm and the next real turn
    fired it again. Measured 2026-09-26: a synthetic turn at 14:55:57 read 0,
    the real turn at 15:04:05 read 309,444, and the forced write went a second
    time with the same reason.
    """
    if not path:
        return None
    total = None
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line:
                    continue
                try:
                    rec = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                msg = rec.get("message") if isinstance(rec, dict) else None
                if not (isinstance(msg, dict) and msg.get("role") == "assistant"):
                    continue
                if not MODEL_ID.match(str(msg.get("model") or "")):
                    continue
                usage = msg.get("usage")
                if not isinstance(usage, dict):
                    continue
                read = usage.get("cache_read_input_tokens")
                made = usage.get("cache_creation_input_tokens")
                if not isinstance(read, int) and not isinstance(made, int):
                    continue
                total = (read if isinstance(read, int) else 0) + (made if isinstance(made, int) else 0)
    except OSError:
        return None
    return total


def parse_window(raw):
    """A window value as a number of tokens, or None.

    The harness takes `auto` and a token count, and writes counts like `500k` by
    hand. `auto` means the model's own limit, which is not a number this program
    can know, so it reads as no threshold rather than as a guess.
    """
    text = str(raw or "").strip().lower()
    if not text or text == "auto":
        return None
    mult = 1
    if text.endswith("k"):
        mult, text = 1000, text[:-1]
    elif text.endswith("m"):
        mult, text = 1000000, text[:-1]
    try:
        value = int(float(text) * mult)
    except ValueError:
        return None
    return value if value > 0 else None


def compaction_window():
    """The automatic compaction window in force here, and where it came from.

    THE ACCOUNT'S OWN SETTINGS FILE IS WHERE THE ANSWER LIVES, because that is
    the scope the harness reads this key in and the scope a person sets it in.
    Provisioning writes none, by the ruling of 2026-09-26.
    Measured 2026-09-26 over three matched background sessions, each filled to
    about 175,000 tokens over fifteen turns with the window asked for at 100,000:
    the key in a user settings file compacted twice, the `--autocompact` flag
    compacted twice, and CLAUDE_CODE_AUTO_COMPACT_WINDOW compacted not at all.

    THE ENVIRONMENT IS READ FIRST ANYWAY, AND NOT BECAUSE IT WORKS. A later
    harness may honour the documented override, and a claw that set it would then
    be running a window this program was not reading. Reading it costs nothing and
    the source string says which answer a pass took, so the two can never disagree
    silently. The machine's policy tier is read for the same reason.

    A window nothing declares is (None, ...). The forced write then reads each
    session against its model's default from MODEL_WINDOWS, and a model that
    table does not name gets no token arm. `--check` says which answer it took.
    """
    env = parse_window(os.environ.get(WINDOW_ENV))
    if env:
        return env, "the environment's %s" % WINDOW_ENV
    for path in (os.path.expanduser("~/.claude/settings.json"), MANAGED_SETTINGS):
        try:
            with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                data = json.load(fh)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        if not isinstance(data, dict):
            continue
        value = parse_window(data.get(WINDOW_KEY))
        if value:
            return value, "%s, %s" % (path, WINDOW_KEY)
        env_block = data.get("env")
        if isinstance(env_block, dict):
            value = parse_window(env_block.get(WINDOW_ENV))
            if value:
                return value, ("%s, env.%s, which this harness does not honour"
                               % (path, WINDOW_ENV))
    return None, "nothing declares one, so each session is read against its model's default"


def token_threshold(window):
    """The token count at which the token arm fires for a window, or None."""
    return int(window * TOKEN_ARM_FRACTION) if window else None


def model_window(model):
    """The harness's default window for a model id, and why, from MODEL_WINDOWS."""
    for prefix, tokens in MODEL_WINDOWS:
        if model and model.startswith(prefix):
            return tokens, "the default for %s" % prefix
    return None, ("no default known for %s, so the token arm is off for it"
                  % (model or "a session whose transcript names no model"))


# ------------------------------------------------------------- the workpaper
def find_workpaper(cwd, recorded=""):
    """The workpaper whose clock answers for a handle, and how it was found.

    Nothing on the bus carries this path. The handle records the directory its
    session started in, and the orchestrate skill files a project's workpaper
    inside the project, which is usually below that directory rather than in it.
    Read on this machine 2026-09-26: one orchestrator's recorded directory had
    thirteen workpapers under it, so a search that took the first would answer
    for the wrong project.

    So: a recorded path wins, then the recorded directory itself, then a search
    that answers only when it finds exactly one. Anything else is unresolved, and
    unresolved is said rather than guessed.
    """
    if recorded:
        if os.path.isfile(recorded):
            return recorded, "recorded in this handle's state file"
        return "", "the recorded path %s is not a file" % recorded
    if not (cwd and os.path.isdir(cwd)):
        return "", "the handle records no directory this account can read"
    direct = os.path.join(cwd, "workpaper.md")
    if os.path.isfile(direct):
        return direct, "workpaper.md in the handle's recorded directory"
    found = []
    base_depth = cwd.rstrip(os.sep).count(os.sep)
    for root, dirs, files in os.walk(cwd):
        depth = root.rstrip(os.sep).count(os.sep) - base_depth
        if depth >= WORKPAPER_SEARCH_DEPTH:
            dirs[:] = []
        else:
            dirs[:] = [d for d in dirs if not d.startswith(".") and d not in WORKPAPER_SKIP]
        if "workpaper.md" in files:
            found.append(os.path.join(root, "workpaper.md"))
        if len(found) > 1:
            break
    if len(found) == 1:
        return found[0], "the one workpaper.md under %s" % cwd
    if not found:
        return "", "no workpaper.md under %s within %d levels" % (cwd, WORKPAPER_SEARCH_DEPTH)
    return "", ("more than one workpaper.md under %s, so --set-workpaper has to say "
                "which: %s" % (cwd, ", ".join(sorted(found))))


def file_stamp(path):
    """A file's modification time, or None when there is no file to read."""
    try:
        return os.path.getmtime(path)
    except OSError:
        return None


# ------------------------------------------------------- the compaction records
def records_path():
    return os.path.join(state_dir(), COMPACTION_RECORDS)


def read_records():
    """Every compaction the hook recorded, newest last, with the bytes it read.

    The byte count is returned because the hook appends while this program
    rewrites. A rewrite that did not account for what arrived in between would
    drop a compaction, which is the one event this file exists to not lose.
    """
    path = records_path()
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as fh:
            raw = fh.read()
    except OSError:
        return [], 0
    out = []
    for line in raw.splitlines():
        line = line.strip()
        if not line:
            continue
        try:
            rec = json.loads(line.decode("utf-8", "replace"))
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if isinstance(rec, dict) and rec.get("session"):
            out.append(rec)
    return out, len(raw)


def rewrite_records(keep, read_bytes):
    """Put back the records still outstanding, and whatever arrived while we read.

    The tail beyond `read_bytes` is appended by the hook after this pass read the
    file, so it is a compaction nobody has considered yet and it survives whole.
    """
    path = records_path()
    tail = b""
    try:
        with open(path, "rb") as fh:
            fh.seek(read_bytes)
            tail = fh.read()
    except OSError:
        tail = b""
    body = b"".join(
        (json.dumps(rec, sort_keys=True) + "\n").encode("utf-8") for rec in keep)
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        tmp = path + ".tmp"
        with open(tmp, "wb") as fh:
            fh.write(body + tail)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except OSError as exc:
        log("could not rewrite %s: %s" % (path, exc))


def record_age_hours(rec):
    try:
        at = datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(str(rec.get("at") or ""))
    except ValueError:
        return None
    if at.tzinfo is None:
        at = at.replace(tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc)
    return (datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc) - at).total_seconds() / 3600.0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------- state
def state_path(handle):
    return os.path.join(state_dir(), "%s.json" % re.sub(r"[^A-Za-z0-9_.-]", "_", handle))


def read_state(handle):
    try:
        with open(state_path(handle), "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            data = json.load(fh)
        return data if isinstance(data, dict) else {}
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}


def write_state(handle, patch):
    data = read_state(handle)
    data.update(patch)
    data["handle"] = handle
    path = state_path(handle)
    try:
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
        tmp = path + ".tmp"
        with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            json.dump(data, fh, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except OSError as exc:
        log("could not write %s: %s" % (path, exc))
    return data


def holds():
    """Every hold this account carries, as (handle, hold) pairs.

    A hold is written per handle. hold_scope() says whether it stops the
    account or that handle alone.
    """
    out = []
    try:
        names = sorted(os.listdir(state_dir()))
    except OSError:
        return out
    for name in names:
        if not name.endswith(".json"):
            continue
        try:
            with open(os.path.join(state_dir(), name), "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                data = json.load(fh)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
        if isinstance(data, dict) and isinstance(data.get("hold"), dict):
            out.append((data.get("handle") or name[:-5], data["hold"]))
    return out


def hold_scope(hold):
    """`account` when a hold stops every handle, `handle` when it stops one.

    A hold written before the scope existed carries none. Its sign-in word
    answers, by the same rule a new hold is written under.
    """
    scope = hold.get("scope")
    if scope in ("account", "handle"):
        return scope
    return "account" if hold.get("signin") == "signed-out" else "handle"


def account_holds():
    """The handles whose hold stops every handle of this account."""
    return [h for h, v in holds() if hold_scope(v) == "account"]


def handle_hold(handle):
    """Whether this handle's own hold keeps it from a resume now: (held, why).

    Only a hold of scope `handle` is read here. It is due again once its
    `retry_after` has passed. A hold that carries none was written before the
    retry existed, and it is due now.
    """
    hold = read_state(handle).get("hold")
    if not isinstance(hold, dict) or hold_scope(hold) != "handle":
        return False, ""
    after = hold.get("retry_after")
    if not isinstance(after, (int, float)) or time.time() >= after:
        return False, ""
    return True, ("%s is held on its own since %s: %s. The rail tries it again at %s, "
                  "and every other handle is resumed as usual"
                  % (handle, hold.get("since") or hold.get("at") or "an unknown time",
                     hold.get("said") or hold.get("reason") or "no reason recorded",
                     hold.get("retry_at") or "the next pass"))


def prune_state(older_than=30 * 86400):
    """Drop state for a handle nothing has written to in a month.

    A handle that is gone leaves its file behind forever otherwise, the way the
    wake rail's dedupe state did before it pruned. Every wake, resume and stop
    rewrites the file, so the modification time is the handle's own activity and
    thirty days is far outside any window a pass reads.

    TWO FILES SURVIVE WHATEVER THEIR AGE. A file carrying a hold is the one
    record that this account or this handle is stopped, and a person may clear
    it a long time after the hold was written. A file
    naming a resumed_session is a session this rail started and has not stopped,
    and removing it would leave that session running with nothing left that
    knows to end it. Age is the wrong reading for both: each says something is
    outstanding, and outstanding does not expire.

    Returns the handles removed, so a pass can say what it dropped.
    """
    cutoff = time.time() - older_than
    dropped = []
    try:
        names = sorted(os.listdir(state_dir()))
    except OSError:
        return dropped
    for name in names:
        if not name.endswith(".json"):
            continue
        path = os.path.join(state_dir(), name)
        try:
            if os.path.getmtime(path) >= cutoff:
                continue
        except OSError:
            continue
        try:
            with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                data = json.load(fh)
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            data = {}
        if not isinstance(data, dict):
            data = {}
        if isinstance(data.get("hold"), dict) or data.get("resumed_session"):
            continue
        try:
            os.remove(path)
        except OSError:
            continue
        dropped.append(data.get("handle") or name[:-5])
    return dropped


# ------------------------------------------------------------------- the acts
def signin_reading():
    """The sign-in check's one word for this account, or "unreadable".

    The check is a sibling program and owns the reading, so the hold and the
    hourly alarm cannot disagree about what the harness said.
    """
    check = beside("commonclaw-signin-check")
    if not os.access(check, os.X_OK):
        return "unreadable"
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run([check, "--read"], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                              stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, timeout=150)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
        return "unreadable"
    word = proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip()
    return word if word in ("signed-in", "signed-out", "never") else "unreadable"


def tell_human(bus_dir, handle, reason, subject="", body="", to="human"):
    """One line to the `human` handle, and nothing else anywhere on the bus.

    The wake rail writes to no bus at all, because a rail that filed its own
    failures into an inbox would raise unread mail and nudge itself forever. This
    program writes to one handle that the rail skips by name and that nobody
    drains. So the line reaches the person who has to sign in, and it starts no
    loop.

    A HOLD'S LINE MAY GO TO ONE MORE HANDLE, through `to`. tell_hold() passes
    the handle ORCHESTRATE_HOLD_NOTIFY names, because nobody reads `human`. That
    handle reads the line as mail, once per hold, so it starts no loop either.

    The body carries the handle and the reason. It never carries a token or a
    path a person did not choose to share. hold_words() is what a hold's reason
    passes through first.

    THE HOLD IS THE DEFAULT SUBJECT BECAUSE IT WAS THE FIRST CALLER. Two later
    ones pass their own: a forced write whose workpaper never moved, and a
    compaction the rail cannot act on. Each is a different ask with a different
    repair, and one subject for all three would put them in one thread.
    """
    bus = beside("bus")
    if not os.access(bus, os.X_OK):
        log("no bus command at %s, so the line about %s reached nobody" % (bus, handle))
        return False
    env = dict(os.environ, SESSION_BUS_DIR=bus_dir)
    subject = subject or "continuity rail: %s is held" % handle
    body = body or (
        "%s could not be resumed: %s. No handle of this account is resumed until "
        "the hold is cleared." % (handle, reason))
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [bus, "send", "session-continuity", to, subject, body],
            env=env, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, timeout=30)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        log("could not tell %s about %s: %s: %s" % (to, handle, type(exc).__name__, exc))
        return False
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        log("bus send to %s about %s exited %d" % (to, handle, proc.returncode))
        return False
    return True


def hold_notify():
    """The second reader of a hold's line, from the conf, or "".

    A value that is not shaped like a handle is said in the journal and not
    sent to. `human` is already the first reader.
    """
    name = conf_value(HOLD_NOTIFY_KEY) or ""
    if not name or name == "human":
        return ""
    if not HANDLE_NAME.match(name):
        log("%s in %s is not a handle, so a hold's line goes to human alone"
            % (HOLD_NOTIFY_KEY, CONF))
        return ""
    return name


def tell_hold(bus_dir, handle, subject, body):
    """A hold's line to `human`, then to the conf's second reader. (human, other).

    The second value is None where the conf names no second reader.
    """
    told = tell_human(bus_dir, handle, "", subject=subject, body=body)
    other = hold_notify()
    if not other:
        return told, None
    return told, tell_human(bus_dir, handle, "", subject=subject, body=body, to=other)


# The harness's refusal for a folder its account has not trusted. Read
# 2026-09-30 from a failed resume's stderr. It ends with a step that names a
# command in backticks and the folder.
UNTRUSTED = "Workspace not trusted"
# What a hold's line drops from the harness's words: a path, and a run of
# characters long enough to be a token. Each is replaced by a plain word.
PATH_IN_TEXT = re.compile(r"(?<![\w.])(?:~|/)[^\s'\"`,;]+")
TOKEN_IN_TEXT = re.compile(r"\b(?:sk-[A-Za-z0-9_-]+|[A-Za-z0-9_-]{24,})")


def hold_words(detail, cwd, me):
    """What a hold's line says: (the harness's reason, the one fix step).

    THE REASON IS THE HARNESS'S FIRST SENTENCE, IN ITS OWN WORDS. The rail's
    detail wraps it as "the harness exited N: ...". A path other than the
    handle's own folder becomes "a path", a long run of characters becomes "a
    hidden value", and a backtick becomes a quote. The handle's folder stays,
    because the person chose it when they registered the handle.

    THE FIX STEP IS THE RAIL'S. For a folder the harness does not trust, it names
    the folder and the account. For anything else it names where to run the
    agent to read the refusal whole.
    """
    text = (detail or "").strip()
    m = re.match(r"^the harness exited -?\d+: (.*)$", text)
    if m:
        text = m.group(1)
    if text.startswith(UNTRUSTED):
        reason = UNTRUSTED
        fix = ("Run the agent once in %s as %s and accept the trust prompt" % (cwd, me))
    else:
        text = re.split(r"(?<=[.!?])\s", text, maxsplit=1)[0].rstrip(".")
        keep = cwd.rstrip("/") if cwd else ""

        def path(mt):
            p = mt.group(0).rstrip(".")
            return mt.group(0) if keep and p.rstrip("/") == keep else "a path"
        text = PATH_IN_TEXT.sub(path, text)
        text = TOKEN_IN_TEXT.sub("a hidden value", text)
        reason = text.replace("`", "'") or "no reason was given"
        fix = ("Run the agent once in %s as %s to read the refusal whole, and fix what it "
               "names" % (cwd or "the handle's folder", me))
    return reason, fix


def nudge(rail, adapter_dir, bus_dir, handle, rec, pid, text=None):
    """Hand one handle to the wake rail's adapter, with one fixed sentence.

    THE DEFAULT TEXT IS THE WAKE RAIL'S OWN, TAKEN FROM THE RAIL. Two copies of
    one law drift, and the rail's `--law` would then prove nothing about what a
    session actually received. The forced write passes one of this program's own
    sentences instead, and `--law` here proves that one.

    The session id is left out of the payload on purpose. A twin found by name is
    the case where the app moved the conversation to a new id, so the adapter's
    equality test against the bus record would refuse the very session that is
    live. What identifies the target here is its own pid and its own socket.
    """
    adapter = os.path.join(adapter_dir, "claude")
    if not os.access(adapter, os.X_OK):
        return {"outcome": "failed", "reason": "no adapter at %s" % adapter}
    payload = {
        "handle": handle, "bus_dir": bus_dir, "session": "", "pid": pid,
        "owner": rec.get("owner") or "", "address": rec.get("address") or "",
        "pane": rec.get("pane") or "", "cwd": rec.get("cwd") or "",
        "unread": 0, "text": text if text is not None else rail.nudge_text(bus_dir),
    }
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [adapter, "deliver"], input=json.dumps(payload).encode("utf-8"),
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, timeout=30)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        return {"outcome": "failed", "reason": "%s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)}
    try:
        return json.loads(proc.stdout.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip() or "{}")
    except ValueError:
        return {"outcome": "failed", "reason": "the adapter spoke something other than JSON"}


def resume(guard, cmd, handle, rec, model, bare):
    """Start the recorded session again, then read whether it is here.

    TWO FORMS, AND THE WRONG ONE MAKES A COPY. A session the harness has already
    run in the background carries its own saved options, and flags passed to a
    resume of it start a copy under a new session id. So a saved session is
    resumed bare and wakes with the name, model and permission flag it already
    has. A session with no saved options takes the flags, which is what sets
    those three the first time.

    The reading afterwards is the same one the twin check makes: a live process
    carrying this session id. The harness's own records answer it, so nothing
    here trusts the exit code of a command that returns before the session is up,
    and a resume that made a copy reads as a failure rather than a success.
    """
    session = rec["session"]
    argv = [cmd, "--bg", "--resume", session]
    if not bare:
        argv += ["--name", handle, "--model", model, "--dangerously-skip-permissions"]
    argv.append(RESUME_TURN)
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            argv, cwd=rec["cwd"], stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
            timeout=SPAWN_TIMEOUT_SECS)
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        return False, "the harness did not return: %s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        tail = [l for l in proc.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").splitlines() if l.strip()]
        return False, "the harness exited %d: %s" % (
            proc.returncode, tail[-1].strip() if tail else "it wrote nothing to stderr")
    time.sleep(ALIVE_AFTER_SECS)
    if guard.generations(session):
        return True, "a process carries session %s" % session[:8]
    return False, "the harness exited 0 and no process carried the session %ds later" % ALIVE_AFTER_SECS


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the stop
def stop_session(cmd, row, session):
    """End one listed session. Returns (ok, the job id used, why not).

    THE JOB ID, NOT THE SESSION ID. The listing carries both, and only the short
    `id` addresses a job: `claude stop <session id>` answers "No job matching".
    Measured 2026-09-25 against a session whose two fields differ only in length,
    so a reader cannot tell them apart by eye.

    A ROW WITH NO JOB ID CANNOT BE STOPPED AT ALL. `claude stop` stops a
    background session, and an interactive one carries no `id`. That is said here
    rather than guessed at by passing the session id, which the harness answers
    with a refusal that reads like a session already gone.
    """
    job = row.get("id")
    if not job:
        return False, "", ("this session carries no job id in the harness listing, so "
                           "the harness has nothing to stop it by")
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run([cmd, "stop", job], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                              stderr=subprocess.PIPE, timeout=60)
        ok = proc.returncode == 0
        why = proc.stderr.decode("utf-8", "replace").strip().splitlines()
    except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as exc:
        ok, why = False, ["%s: %s" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)]
    return ok, job, (why[-1] if why else "the harness gave no reason")


def stop_pass(cmd, listing, idle_stop_minutes, act):
    """End the sessions this rail resumed that have gone quiet.

    A resumed session does not exit here. Measured 2026-09-25: one sat idle for 29
    minutes holding 351 MB and was still there. So the rail ends what it started,
    and an orchestrator's resting state stays what the design says it is, a
    registration and a workpaper with no process.

    ONLY WHAT THIS RAIL RESUMED, AND ONLY ONCE. The state file names the session
    each resume started, and a stop clears that name. Nothing else on the board is
    touched: a session somebody else started is somebody else's.

    QUIET MEANS TWO READINGS, NOT ONE. The harness's listing says idle, and the
    transcript has not grown for the threshold. The listing alone would end a
    session between two turns of work it is in the middle of.

    `claude stop` keeps the conversation, so the next pass resumes the same
    session from the same transcript. Nothing is destroyed.
    """
    out = []
    try:
        names = sorted(os.listdir(state_dir()))
    except OSError:
        return out
    for name in names:
        if not name.endswith(".json"):
            continue
        data = read_state(name[:-5])
        session = data.get("resumed_session")
        if not isinstance(session, str) or not session:
            continue
        handle = data.get("handle") or name[:-5]
        item = {"handle": handle, "session": session}
        row = (listing or {}).get(session)
        if listing is None:
            item["outcome"], item["detail"] = "waiting", "the harness's listing could not be read"
            out.append(item)
            continue
        if row is None or not row.get("pid"):
            item["outcome"] = "ended"
            item["detail"] = "it had already ended, so there was nothing to stop"
            if act:
                write_state(handle, {"resumed_session": None, "stopped_at": now_iso(),
                                     "stop_detail": item["detail"]})
            out.append(item)
            continue
        if row.get("status") != "idle":
            item["outcome"], item["detail"] = "working", "the listing reads %s" % row.get("status")
            out.append(item)
            continue
        path = transcript_path(session)
        quiet = None
        try:
            quiet = (time.time() - os.path.getmtime(path)) / 60.0 if path else None
        except OSError:
            quiet = None
        if quiet is None:
            item["outcome"] = "waiting"
            item["detail"] = "no transcript for it, so nothing says how long it has been quiet"
            out.append(item)
            continue
        item["quiet_minutes"] = round(quiet, 1)
        if quiet < idle_stop_minutes:
            item["outcome"] = "waiting"
            item["detail"] = ("quiet %.1f of the %d minutes" % (quiet, idle_stop_minutes))
            out.append(item)
            continue
        if not act:
            item["outcome"], item["detail"] = "would stop", "quiet %.1f minutes" % quiet
            out.append(item)
            continue
        ok, job, why = stop_session(cmd, row, session)
        item["job"] = job
        item["outcome"] = "stopped" if ok else "failed"
        item["detail"] = ("quiet %.1f minutes, the conversation is kept" % quiet if ok
                          else why)
        if ok:
            write_state(handle, {"resumed_session": None, "stopped_at": now_iso(),
                                 "stop_detail": item["detail"]})
        log("%s: stop %s -- %s" % (handle, item["outcome"], item["detail"]))
        out.append(item)
    return out


# ------------------------------------------------------------------- the pass
def survey(guard, listing, bus_dir, me, only=(), read_all=False):
    """One row per orchestrator handle this account holds, with what a pass owes it.

    Every such handle is listed, including the quiet ones, because `--check` is
    read to learn what the rail covers and a handle left out of it reads exactly
    like a handle the rail does not know about.

    `only` narrows what a pass acts on, and nothing else. The rows outside it are
    still listed and still carry the action they would have taken, so the reading
    is of the whole board whatever the pass touches.

    `read_all` reads rule A for every row with a session. `--check` passes it. An
    acting pass reads rule A only for a row it would resume.
    """
    rows = []
    try:
        with open(os.path.join(bus_dir, "handles.json"), "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            handles = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
        return rows, "cannot read the board at %s: %s" % (bus_dir, exc)
    if not isinstance(handles, dict):
        return rows, "the board at %s is not a JSON object" % bus_dir

    live = guard.live_records()
    saved = saved_sessions(listing)
    for handle, rec in sorted(handles.items()):
        if not isinstance(rec, dict) or rec.get("role") != ROLE or rec.get("owner") != me:
            continue
        unread, newest = unread_count(bus_dir, handle)
        row = {"handle": handle, "session": rec.get("session") or "", "unread": unread,
               "newest_id": newest, "address": rec.get("address") or "",
               "cwd": rec.get("cwd") or ""}
        rows.append(row)
        # THE TWIN READING IS TAKEN FOR EVERY HANDLE, MAIL OR NONE. It answers
        # whether the session is here, and two later passes need that answer
        # about a handle with an empty inbox: a live orchestrator is the one the
        # forced write is delivered to, and the one a compaction record names.
        # It used to be read inside the mail branch, where those passes could
        # not see it.
        carriers = guard.generations(row["session"], live) if row["session"] else []
        wanted = {handle, row["address"], LISTING_REF.sub("", row["address"])} - {""}
        named = [r for r in live
                 if r["name"] in wanted and r["session"] != row["session"]]
        if carriers:
            row["live_pid"] = carriers[-1]["pid"]
            row["live_session"] = row["session"]
        elif named:
            row["live_pid"] = named[-1]["pid"]
            row["live_session"] = named[-1]["session"]
        # --check READS RULE A FOR EVERY ROW, so a person sees what a resume would
        # meet. An acting pass reads it only where it would resume.
        if read_all and row["session"]:
            row["rule_a"] = conversation_reading(row["session"])
            if row["rule_a"]["origin"] == APP_ENTRYPOINT:
                row["rule_c"] = moved_reading(row["session"])
        if unread <= 0:
            row["action"], row["reason"] = "idle", "no mail, nothing to do"
            continue
        if not row["session"]:
            row["action"] = "refused"
            row["reason"] = "the record carries no session id, so there is nothing to resume"
            continue
        if not (os.path.isdir(row["cwd"]) and os.access(row["cwd"], os.X_OK)):
            row["action"] = "refused"
            row["reason"] = ("the recorded directory %s is not one this account can enter"
                             % (row["cwd"] or "<none>"))
            continue
        if carriers:
            top = carriers[-1]
            row["action"] = "nudge"
            row["pid"] = top["pid"]
            row["reason"] = ("session %s already runs in pid %d, so the session is here"
                             % (row["session"][:8], top["pid"]))
            continue
        if named:
            top = named[-1]
            row["action"] = "nudge"
            row["pid"] = top["pid"]
            row["reason"] = ("pid %d carries the name '%s' under session %s, so the "
                             "conversation moved and the session is here"
                             % (top["pid"], top["name"], top["session"][:8]))
            continue
        if "rule_a" not in row:
            row["rule_a"] = conversation_reading(row["session"])
        if row["rule_a"]["verdict"] == "refused":
            row["action"], row["reason"] = "refused", row["rule_a"]["reason"]
            continue
        # RULE C, FOR THE APP'S CONVERSATIONS. The app never moves a conversation
        # the command line started, so those resume as before.
        target = row["session"]
        if row["rule_a"]["origin"] == APP_ENTRYPOINT:
            if "rule_c" not in row:
                row["rule_c"] = moved_reading(row["session"])
            moved = row["rule_c"]
            if moved["verdict"] == "refused":
                row["action"], row["reason"] = "refused", moved["reason"]
                continue
            if moved["verdict"] == "newer session":
                target = moved["newest"]
                here_now = guard.generations(target, live)
                if here_now:
                    row["action"] = "nudge"
                    row["pid"] = here_now[-1]["pid"]
                    row["reason"] = ("the app moved the conversation to session %s, and pid %d "
                                     "carries it" % (target[:8], row["pid"]))
                    continue
                # The newest session answers rule A on its own, as the recorded
                # one did: a copy is refused wherever it sits in the line.
                newest_a = conversation_reading(target)
                if newest_a["verdict"] == "refused":
                    row["action"], row["reason"] = "refused", newest_a["reason"]
                    continue
        row["resume_session"] = target
        # WHICH RESUME FORM. A listing that could not be read decides nothing,
        # and a flagged resume of a saved session is the one outcome nothing
        # later can undo, so an unreadable listing is refused.
        if saved is None:
            row["action"] = "refused"
            row["reason"] = ("the harness's session listing could not be read, so nothing "
                             "says which resume form this session takes")
            continue
        row["bare"] = target in saved
        row["action"] = "resume"
        row["reason"] = "no process carries the session and no live session carries its name"
        if target != row["session"]:
            row["reason"] = ("the app moved the conversation to session %s, and no process carries "
                             "it, so the rail resumes that one" % target[:8])
        if row["bare"]:
            row["model_source"] = "the session's saved options"
            continue
        model, path = transcript_model(target)
        row["model"] = model or (conf_value("ORCHESTRATE_DELEGATE_MODEL") or "")
        row["model_source"] = "the transcript" if model else CONF
        row["transcript"] = path
        if not row["model"]:
            row["action"] = "refused"
            row["reason"] = ("no model: this session carries no saved options, the transcript "
                             "records none and %s names no ORCHESTRATE_DELEGATE_MODEL" % CONF)
    return rows, ""


# --------------------------------------------------------- the forced write
def credit_activity(handle, data, size, act):
    """Add this pass's share of activity, and say how much is banked.

    ACTIVITY IS THE TRANSCRIPT GROWING, NOT THE CLOCK. A session nobody is using
    accrues none, which is the whole point: an orchestrator that sat quiet for a
    week does not owe a write the moment somebody says hello to it. The gap since
    the last reading is credited only when the file grew, and only up to
    ACTIVITY_CREDIT_CAP_SECS of it, so a rail that was off for a day cannot bank
    the day as work.

    The reading is written back on an acting pass only. `--check` returns the same
    numbers and moves nothing, which is what makes it safe to run by hand.
    """
    prior = data.get("activity") if isinstance(data.get("activity"), dict) else {}
    banked = float(prior.get("seconds") or 0.0)
    last_size = prior.get("size")
    last_at = float(prior.get("at") or 0.0)
    now = time.time()
    grew = isinstance(last_size, int) and size > last_size
    if grew and last_at:
        banked += min(now - last_at, float(ACTIVITY_CREDIT_CAP_SECS))
    if act:
        write_state(handle, {"activity": {"size": size, "at": now, "seconds": banked}})
    return banked, grew


def forced_write_pass(rail, adapter_dir, bus_dir, rows, activity_hours, window, only, act,
                      compacting=()):
    """Order the postures write where a live orchestrator is due one, then read the stamp.

    TWO ARMS, AND EITHER ONE IS ENOUGH. The cadence arm counts the handle's own
    activity. The token arm reads the transcript's last turn against
    TOKEN_ARM_FRACTION of the automatic compaction window, and fires once per
    crossing per session: a pass that reads below the threshold re-arms it,
    which is what a compaction does.
    Without that re-arm every pass past the threshold would deliver the same turn.

    THE ARM IS KEYED ON THE SESSION ID, NOT ON THE PROCESS. The app gives a
    conversation a new process and keeps its id, and that is the same crossing.
    A conversation under a new session id is a new transcript and a new crossing,
    so it re-arms.

    THE STAMP IS READ ON A LATER PASS, NOT IN THIS ONE. The turn has to reach the
    session, the session has to take a turn, and the write has to land. So the
    workpaper's clock is recorded when the turn goes out and compared
    STAMP_GRACE_MINUTES later.
    """
    out = []
    for row in rows:
        handle = row["handle"]
        pid = row.get("live_pid")
        session = row.get("live_session") or row["session"]
        item = {"handle": handle}
        out.append(item)
        if handle in compacting:
            # A HANDLE WITH A COMPACTION OUTSTANDING IS NOT NUDGED. The resume
            # the other pass owes it re-invokes the skill and runs its resume
            # operation, which writes more than this turn asks for. A nudge here
            # would also be delivered to a pid that pass is about to stop.
            item["outcome"] = "skipped"
            item["detail"] = "a compaction of this handle is outstanding, and the resume covers it"
            continue
        if not pid or not session:
            item["outcome"], item["detail"] = "idle", "no live session to write anything"
            continue
        path = transcript_path(session)
        if not path:
            item["outcome"] = "waiting"
            item["detail"] = "no transcript for session %s, so nothing says how busy it is" % session[:8]
            continue
        try:
            size = os.path.getsize(path)
        except OSError:
            item["outcome"], item["detail"] = "waiting", "the transcript could not be read"
            continue
        data = read_state(handle)
        banked, grew = credit_activity(handle, data, size, act)
        item["activity_minutes"] = round(banked / 60.0, 1)
        item["grew"] = grew

        tokens = transcript_tokens(path)
        item["tokens"] = tokens
        # A window somebody declared wins. Otherwise each session is read
        # against its own model's default, because one account runs several.
        row_window, item["window_source"] = window, "declared"
        if not row_window:
            row_window, item["window_source"] = model_window(transcript_model(session)[0])
        item["window"] = row_window
        threshold = token_threshold(row_window)
        item["token_threshold"] = threshold
        armed = data.get("token_arm_armed")
        armed = True if armed is None else bool(armed)
        fired_in = data.get("token_arm_session")
        if not armed and fired_in and fired_in != session:
            armed = True
            if act:
                write_state(handle, {"token_arm_armed": True, "token_arm_session": None})
        if threshold and isinstance(tokens, int) and tokens < threshold and not armed:
            armed = True
            if act:
                write_state(handle, {"token_arm_armed": True})

        due = ""
        if banked >= activity_hours * 3600:
            due = "%.1f hours of activity since the last forced write" % (banked / 3600.0)
        elif threshold and isinstance(tokens, int) and tokens >= threshold and armed:
            due = ("the last turn read %d tokens, past %d%% of the %d-token window"
                   % (tokens, round(TOKEN_ARM_FRACTION * 100), row_window))
        if not due:
            item["outcome"] = "idle"
            item["detail"] = ("nothing due: %.1f of %d activity hours, %s"
                              % (banked / 3600.0, activity_hours,
                                 "no token threshold" if not threshold
                                 else "%s tokens against %d" % (tokens, threshold)))
            continue
        item["due"] = due
        if only and handle not in only:
            item["outcome"] = "skipped"
            item["detail"] = "this pass was scoped to %s" % ", ".join(sorted(only))
            continue
        if not act:
            item["outcome"], item["detail"] = "would write", due
            continue
        book, how = find_workpaper(row.get("cwd") or "", data.get("workpaper") or "")
        got = nudge(rail, adapter_dir, bus_dir, handle,
                    {"owner": "", "address": row.get("address") or "", "cwd": row.get("cwd") or ""},
                    pid, text=FORCED_WRITE_TURN)
        item["outcome"] = got.get("outcome", "failed")
        item["detail"] = got.get("reason") or got.get("detail") or ""
        item["workpaper"] = book
        item["workpaper_how"] = how
        patch = {"last_forced_write": now_iso(), "last_forced_write_reason": due,
                 "last_forced_write_outcome": item["outcome"],
                 "workpaper_resolved": book, "workpaper_how": how}
        if item["outcome"] == "delivered":
            # The cadence starts again from zero, and the token arm waits for a
            # reading below the threshold before it may fire again.
            patch["activity"] = {"size": size, "at": time.time(), "seconds": 0.0}
            patch["token_arm_armed"] = False
            patch["token_arm_session"] = session
            patch["stamp_at_write"] = file_stamp(book) if book else None
            patch["stamp_told_human"] = False
        write_state(handle, patch)
        log("%s: forced write %s -- %s" % (handle, item["outcome"], due))
    return out


# The two turns whose stamp is read afterwards, and the state keys each one
# leaves: when it went out, whether it was delivered, the workpaper, its clock at
# the time, and whether the human has been told.
STAMPED_TURNS = (
    ("write", "last_forced_write", "last_forced_write_outcome", "workpaper_resolved",
     "workpaper_how", "stamp_at_write", "stamp_told_human", "stamp_outcome"),
    ("resume", "post_compact_turn_at", None, "post_compact_workpaper",
     "post_compact_workpaper_how", "post_compact_stamp_before", "post_compact_stamp_told",
     "post_compact_stamp_outcome"),
)


def stamp_pass(bus_dir, rows, act):
    """Read whether the workpaper moved after a turn the rail sent, and say so once.

    TWO TURNS ARE READ THIS WAY. The forced write asks for the postures; the
    post-compaction phrase runs the resume operation, which records itself in the
    workpaper. Either one that left the workpaper still for the grace period is
    one line in the human's queue, and the state file records that the line went
    so the next pass does not send it again. A workpaper the search could not
    name is reported the same way and the check is skipped, because a stamp
    nothing read is not a stamp that failed.
    """
    out = []
    for row in rows:
        handle = row["handle"]
        data = read_state(handle)
        for turn, at_key, outcome_key, book_key, how_key, before_key, told_key, result_key \
                in STAMPED_TURNS:
            wrote = data.get(at_key)
            if not wrote:
                continue
            if outcome_key and data.get(outcome_key) != "delivered":
                continue
            if data.get(told_key):
                continue
            try:
                at = datetime.datetime.fromisoformat(str(wrote))
            except ValueError:
                continue
            if at.tzinfo is None:
                at = at.replace(tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc)
            waited = (datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc) - at).total_seconds() / 60.0
            item = {"handle": handle, "turn": turn, "waited_minutes": round(waited, 1)}
            if waited < STAMP_GRACE_MINUTES:
                item["outcome"] = "waiting"
                item["detail"] = "%.1f of the %d minutes" % (waited, STAMP_GRACE_MINUTES)
                out.append(item)
                continue
            book = data.get(book_key) or ""
            before = data.get(before_key)
            now_stamp = file_stamp(book) if book else None
            if book and isinstance(before, (int, float)) and isinstance(now_stamp, (int, float)) \
                    and now_stamp > before:
                item["outcome"] = "moved"
                item["detail"] = "%s was written after the turn went out" % book
                if act:
                    write_state(handle, {told_key: True, result_key: "moved"})
                out.append(item)
                continue
            if not book:
                reason = ("the rail could not name this handle's workpaper, so nothing "
                          "read whether the write happened: %s" % (data.get(how_key) or ""))
            else:
                reason = "%s has not been written since the turn went out" % book
            item["outcome"] = "missed"
            item["detail"] = reason
            out.append(item)
            if not act:
                continue
            if turn == "write":
                subject = "continuity rail: %s did not write its postures" % handle
                body = ("%s was told to write its standing postures and its skills in use to "
                        "the workpaper %s minutes ago, and %s. Run 'session-continuity "
                        "--set-workpaper %s <path>' where the path is what is wrong."
                        % (handle, int(waited), reason.rstrip("."), handle))
            else:
                subject = "continuity rail: %s did not record its resume" % handle
                body = ("%s was sent '%s' after a compaction %s minutes ago, and %s. The resume "
                        "operation records itself in the workpaper, so either it did not run or "
                        "the rail reads the wrong file. Run 'session-continuity --set-workpaper "
                        "%s <path>' where the path is what is wrong."
                        % (handle, RESUME_TURN or "the resume phrase", int(waited),
                           reason.rstrip("."), handle))
            told = tell_human(bus_dir, handle, reason, subject=subject, body=body)
            write_state(handle, {told_key: True, result_key: "missed",
                                 told_key + "_ok": told})
            log("%s: %s stamp missed -- %s" % (handle, turn, reason))
    return out


# ------------------------------------------------- the post-compaction resume
def compaction_pass(guard, rail, adapter_dir, cmd, listing, bus_dir, rows, only, act, held=None):
    """Re-ground an orchestrator the hook recorded a compaction for.

    TWO ROADS, CHOSEN BY WHETHER THE SESSION IS HERE. Q184, ruled 2026-09-26.

      live  RESUME_TURN is written into the session through the wake rail's
            adapter, the road a nudge takes. The orchestrate skill's resume hook
            matches the phrase on the receiving side and puts the skill's front
            page in front of the model before its first token. Nothing is stopped, so a
            desktop session with no job id takes this road like any other.
      gone  the session is resumed with RESUME_TURN as its prompt, which the
            harness expands into the skill. The form is the mail road's: bare
            for a session with a background row, flagged for one with none.

    THE STAMP IS READ AFTERWARDS ON BOTH ROADS. The resume operation records
    itself in the workpaper, so a workpaper that has not moved STAMP_GRACE_MINUTES
    after the turn is one line in the human's queue. stamp_pass reads it.

    A RECORD IS CONSUMED ONLY BY AN ACT OR BY AGE. One that cannot be acted on yet
    is left, so the next pass finds it. A failed resume writes the same hold the
    mail road writes, so a signed-out harness is asked about once and not every
    two minutes.

    `held` is the handles whose hold stops the account. A handle's own hold is
    read here as the mail road reads it.
    """
    held = held if held is not None else []
    records, read_bytes = read_records()
    out, touched = [], set()
    if not records:
        return out, touched
    by_handle = {}
    for row in rows:
        for sid in {row.get("session"), row.get("live_session")} - {None, ""}:
            by_handle.setdefault(sid, row)
    saved = saved_sessions(listing)

    keep = []
    for rec in records:
        session = str(rec.get("session") or "")
        age = record_age_hours(rec)
        item = {"session": session[:8], "at": rec.get("at")}
        row = by_handle.get(session)
        if age is not None and age > RECORD_TTL_HOURS:
            item["outcome"] = "dropped"
            item["detail"] = "the record is %.1f hours old, past the %d-hour life" % (age, RECORD_TTL_HOURS)
            out.append(item)
            continue
        if row is None:
            # Not an orchestrator of this account. The hook records every
            # compaction on the machine, because it cannot ask the bus what a
            # session is without becoming a program that can fail a session.
            item["outcome"] = "not ours"
            item["detail"] = "no orchestrator handle of this account records session %s" % session[:8]
            out.append(item)
            keep.append(rec)
            continue
        handle = row["handle"]
        item["handle"] = handle
        touched.add(handle)
        cwd = row.get("cwd") or ""
        if not (os.path.isdir(cwd) and os.access(cwd, os.X_OK)):
            item["outcome"] = "refused"
            item["detail"] = ("the recorded directory %s is not one this account can enter"
                              % (cwd or "<none>"))
            out.append(item)
            keep.append(rec)
            continue
        if only and handle not in only:
            item["outcome"] = "skipped"
            item["detail"] = "this pass was scoped to %s" % ", ".join(sorted(only))
            out.append(item)
            keep.append(rec)
            continue
        pid = row.get("live_pid")
        item["road"] = "live" if pid else "gone"
        if RESUME_TURN is None:
            item["outcome"] = "refused"
            item["detail"] = no_phrase_detail()
            out.append(item)
            keep.append(rec)
            if act:
                log("%s: post-compaction refused -- %s" % (handle, item["detail"]))
            continue

        # ---- live: the phrase through the adapter ----
        if pid:
            if not act:
                item["outcome"] = "would deliver"
                item["detail"] = "pid %d carries the session, so the phrase goes to it" % pid
                out.append(item)
                keep.append(rec)
                continue
            got = nudge(rail, adapter_dir, bus_dir, handle,
                        {"owner": "", "address": row.get("address") or "", "cwd": cwd},
                        pid, text=RESUME_TURN)
            item["outcome"] = got.get("outcome", "failed")
            item["detail"] = got.get("reason") or got.get("detail") or ""
            out.append(item)
            patch = {"last_post_compact": now_iso(), "last_post_compact_outcome": item["outcome"],
                     "last_post_compact_detail": item["detail"], "last_post_compact_road": "live"}
            if item["outcome"] == "delivered":
                patch.update(post_compact_stamp(read_state(handle), cwd))
            else:
                keep.append(rec)
            write_state(handle, patch)
            log("%s: post-compaction phrase %s -- %s" % (handle, item["outcome"], item["detail"]))
            continue

        # ---- gone: a resume with the phrase as its prompt ----
        reading = conversation_reading(session)
        item["rule_a"] = reading
        if reading["verdict"] == "refused":
            item["outcome"], item["detail"] = "refused", reading["reason"]
            out.append(item)
            keep.append(rec)
            if act:
                log("%s: post-compaction refused -- %s" % (handle, item["detail"]))
            continue
        # RULE C ON THE GONE ROAD. A newer session of the app's conversation
        # takes the phrase: through the adapter when it is live, by a resume
        # when it is not. The recorded session is never resumed past it.
        if reading["origin"] == APP_ENTRYPOINT:
            moved = moved_reading(session)
            item["rule_c"] = moved
            newest_a = (conversation_reading(moved["newest"])
                        if moved["verdict"] == "newer session" else None)
            why = (moved["reason"] if moved["verdict"] == "refused" else
                   newest_a["reason"] if newest_a and newest_a["verdict"] == "refused" else "")
            if why:
                item["outcome"], item["detail"] = "refused", why
                out.append(item)
                keep.append(rec)
                if act:
                    log("%s: post-compaction refused -- %s" % (handle, why))
                continue
            if moved["verdict"] == "newer session":
                session = moved["newest"]
                item["resume_session"] = session[:8]
                here_now = guard.generations(session)
                if here_now:
                    item["road"] = "live"
                    pid = here_now[-1]["pid"]
                    if not act:
                        item["outcome"] = "would deliver"
                        item["detail"] = ("the app moved the conversation to session %s, and pid %d "
                                          "carries it, so the phrase goes to it" % (session[:8], pid))
                        out.append(item)
                        keep.append(rec)
                        continue
                    got = nudge(rail, adapter_dir, bus_dir, handle,
                                {"owner": "", "address": row.get("address") or "", "cwd": cwd},
                                pid, text=RESUME_TURN)
                    item["outcome"] = got.get("outcome", "failed")
                    item["detail"] = got.get("reason") or got.get("detail") or ""
                    out.append(item)
                    patch = {"last_post_compact": now_iso(),
                             "last_post_compact_outcome": item["outcome"],
                             "last_post_compact_detail": item["detail"],
                             "last_post_compact_road": "live"}
                    if item["outcome"] == "delivered":
                        patch.update(post_compact_stamp(read_state(handle), cwd))
                    else:
                        keep.append(rec)
                    write_state(handle, patch)
                    log("%s: post-compaction phrase %s -- %s" % (handle, item["outcome"], item["detail"]))
                    continue
        if saved is None:
            item["outcome"] = "waiting"
            item["detail"] = ("the harness's session listing could not be read, so nothing says "
                              "which resume form this session takes")
            out.append(item)
            keep.append(rec)
            continue
        bare = session in saved
        model = ""
        if not bare:
            model, _ = transcript_model(session)
            model = model or (conf_value("ORCHESTRATE_DELEGATE_MODEL") or "")
            if not model:
                item["outcome"] = "refused"
                item["detail"] = ("no model: this session carries no saved options, the transcript "
                                  "records none and %s names no ORCHESTRATE_DELEGATE_MODEL" % CONF)
                out.append(item)
                keep.append(rec)
                continue
        item["bare"] = bare
        if held:
            item["outcome"] = "held"
            item["detail"] = ("this account is held by %s, and no handle is resumed until "
                              "--clear-hold clears it" % ", ".join(held))
            out.append(item)
            keep.append(rec)
            continue
        own, why = handle_hold(handle)
        if own:
            item["outcome"], item["detail"] = "held", why
            out.append(item)
            keep.append(rec)
            continue
        if not act:
            item["outcome"] = "would resume"
            item["detail"] = ("no process carries the session; a %s resume with the phrase as "
                              "its prompt" % ("bare" if bare else "flagged"))
            out.append(item)
            keep.append(rec)
            continue
        resumed, detail = resume(guard, cmd, handle, {"session": session, "cwd": cwd}, model, bare)
        item["outcome"] = "resumed" if resumed else "failed"
        item["detail"] = detail
        out.append(item)
        patch = {"last_post_compact": now_iso(), "last_post_compact_outcome": item["outcome"],
                 "last_post_compact_detail": detail, "last_post_compact_road": "gone"}
        if resumed:
            # What this rail started is what this rail ends: the stop pass reads
            # this name once the session goes quiet.
            patch["resumed_session"] = session
            patch["stopped_at"] = None
            patch["hold"] = None
            if isinstance(read_state(handle).get("hold"), dict):
                patch["hold_healed_at"] = now_iso()
            patch.update(post_compact_stamp(read_state(handle), cwd))
        else:
            patch.update(hold_patch(bus_dir, handle, detail, model, cwd))
            if hold_scope(patch["hold"]) == "account":
                held.append(handle)
            keep.append(rec)
        write_state(handle, patch)
        log("%s: post-compaction %s -- %s" % (handle, item["outcome"], detail))

    if act and len(keep) != len(records):
        rewrite_records(keep, read_bytes)
    return out, touched


def post_compact_stamp(data, cwd):
    """The state a post-compaction turn leaves for stamp_pass to read later."""
    book, how = find_workpaper(cwd, data.get("workpaper") or "")
    return {"post_compact_turn_at": now_iso(), "post_compact_workpaper": book,
            "post_compact_workpaper_how": how,
            "post_compact_stamp_before": file_stamp(book) if book else None,
            "post_compact_stamp_told": False}


def hold_patch(bus_dir, handle, detail, model, cwd):
    """The hold a failed resume writes, and the one line that says so.

    BOTH RESUME ROADS WRITE THIS, and one function is what keeps them saying the
    same thing. A resume that failed because the harness is signed out holds the
    account, because every resume would fail the same way. Any other failure
    holds this handle alone, and the rail tries it again after HOLD_RETRY_SECS.

    A RETRY THAT FAILS THE SAME WAY SAYS NOTHING NEW. The line went out with the
    first failure. A new line goes when the scope or the reason changed.

    THE ID IS THE HOLD, FOR THE HOLD CHECK. A retry keeps it, and a hold
    written after a heal gets a new one, so root sends once per hold. `since`
    cannot be the key: it is whole seconds, and a heal and a new hold can share
    one.
    """
    signin = signin_reading()
    me = pwd.getpwuid(os.geteuid()).pw_name
    prior = read_state(handle).get("hold")
    prior = prior if isinstance(prior, dict) else {}
    scope = "account" if signin == "signed-out" else "handle"
    reason, fix = hold_words(detail, cwd, me)
    now = time.time()
    hold = {"at": now_iso(), "reason": detail, "said": reason, "model": model,
            "signin": signin, "scope": scope,
            "kind": "sign-out" if scope == "account" else "resume-failed",
            "id": prior.get("id") or uuid.uuid4().hex[:16],
            "class": "untrusted" if reason == UNTRUSTED else "refused",
            "cwd": cwd or "",
            "since": prior.get("since") or prior.get("at") or now_iso(),
            "tries": int(prior.get("tries") or 0) + 1 if prior else 1}
    if scope == "handle":
        hold["retry_after"] = now + HOLD_RETRY_SECS
        hold["retry_at"] = datetime.datetime.fromtimestamp(
            now + HOLD_RETRY_SECS, datetime.timezone.utc).replace(microsecond=0).isoformat()
    patch = {"hold": hold}
    if prior and hold_scope(prior) == scope and prior.get("said") == reason:
        return patch
    if scope == "account":
        subject = "continuity rail: %s is held, and so is every handle of %s" % (handle, me)
        body = ("%s could not be resumed because the harness is signed out for %s. No "
                "handle of %s is resumed until the hold is cleared. Somebody runs /login "
                "as %s, then 'session-continuity --clear-hold %s'." % (handle, me, me, me, handle))
    else:
        subject = "continuity rail: %s is held" % handle
        body = ("%s could not be resumed: %s. %s. The rail tries %s again within the hour, "
                "and every other handle is resumed as usual."
                % (handle, reason, fix, handle))
    body = " ".join(body.replace("`", "'").split())
    patch["hold_told_human"], patch["hold_told_notify"] = tell_hold(bus_dir, handle, subject, body)
    return patch


# ------------------------------------------------------------------- the pass
def run_pass(guard, rail, adapter_dir, cmd, bus_dir, me, only=(), idle_stop_minutes=IDLE_STOP_MINUTES,
             activity_hours=ACTIVITY_HOURS, act=True):
    listing = listed_sessions(cmd)
    rows, why = survey(guard, listing, bus_dir, me, only, read_all=not act)
    window, window_source = compaction_window()
    # `held` NAMES THE HOLDS THAT STOP THE ACCOUNT. A handle's own hold is read
    # where that handle would be resumed, and it stops nothing else.
    result = {"bus_dir": bus_dir, "user": me, "only": sorted(only),
              "idle_stop_minutes": idle_stop_minutes, "activity_hours": activity_hours,
              "window": window, "window_source": window_source,
              "held": account_holds(), "rows": rows,
              "stops": stop_pass(cmd, listing, idle_stop_minutes, act)}
    # A BOARD THIS PROGRAM CANNOT READ ENDS THE PASS BEFORE ANY OTHER READING.
    # The three passes below each decide from a handle row, and a pass with no
    # rows would report every compaction as belonging to nobody and rewrite the
    # record file on that reading.
    if why:
        result["blocking"] = [why]
        return result, 3
    # THE COMPACTION ROAD RUNS FIRST, AND WHAT IT TOUCHED THE OTHER TWO LEAVE
    # ALONE. It delivers the resume phrase or starts the session again, and a
    # forced write or a mail nudge on top of that would be a second turn racing
    # the resume.
    result["compactions"], compacting = compaction_pass(guard, rail, adapter_dir, cmd, listing,
                                                        bus_dir, rows, only, act,
                                                        result["held"])
    result["writes"] = forced_write_pass(rail, adapter_dir, bus_dir, rows, activity_hours,
                                         window, only, act, compacting)
    result["stamps"] = stamp_pass(bus_dir, rows, act)
    if not act:
        return result, 0
    for row in rows:
        if row["action"] not in ("resume", "nudge"):
            continue
        handle = row["handle"]
        if handle in compacting:
            row["outcome"] = "skipped"
            row["detail"] = ("the compaction road acted on this handle in this pass, so its "
                             "mail is read by the session that road resumed")
            continue
        if only and handle not in only:
            row["outcome"] = "skipped"
            row["detail"] = "this pass was scoped to %s" % ", ".join(sorted(only))
            continue
        if row["action"] == "nudge":
            got = nudge(rail, adapter_dir, bus_dir, handle, {"owner": me,
                        "address": row["address"], "cwd": row["cwd"]}, row["pid"])
            row["outcome"] = got.get("outcome", "failed")
            row["detail"] = got.get("reason") or got.get("detail") or ""
            write_state(handle, {"last_wake": now_iso(), "last_wake_outcome": row["outcome"],
                                 "last_wake_detail": row["detail"]})
            log("%s: nudged pid %d -- %s %s" % (handle, row["pid"], row["outcome"], row["detail"]))
            continue
        if result["held"]:
            row["action"], row["outcome"] = "held", "skipped"
            row["detail"] = ("this account is held by %s, and no handle is resumed until "
                             "--clear-hold clears it" % ", ".join(result["held"]))
            log("%s: %s" % (handle, row["detail"]))
            continue
        own, why = handle_hold(handle)
        if own:
            row["action"], row["outcome"], row["detail"] = "held", "skipped", why
            log("%s: %s" % (handle, why))
            continue
        if RESUME_TURN is None:
            row["outcome"], row["detail"] = "refused", no_phrase_detail()
            log("%s: resume refused -- %s" % (handle, row["detail"]))
            continue
        # Rule C may name a newer session than the one the handle records, and
        # that is the one resumed. It registers the handle from inside.
        target = row.get("resume_session") or row["session"]
        ok, detail = resume(guard, cmd, handle, {"session": target, "cwd": row["cwd"]},
                            row.get("model") or "", row["bare"])
        row["outcome"] = "resumed" if ok else "failed"
        row["detail"] = detail
        patch = {"last_resume": now_iso(), "last_resume_outcome": row["outcome"],
                 "last_resume_detail": detail, "last_resume_model": row.get("model") or "",
                 "last_resume_bare": row["bare"], "last_resume_session": target}
        if ok:
            patch["hold"] = None
            if isinstance(read_state(handle).get("hold"), dict):
                patch["hold_healed_at"] = now_iso()
            # What this rail started is what this rail ends. The stop pass reads
            # this name, and a stop clears it.
            patch["resumed_session"] = target
            patch["stopped_at"] = None
        else:
            patch.update(hold_patch(bus_dir, handle, detail, row.get("model") or "", row["cwd"]))
            if hold_scope(patch["hold"]) == "account":
                result["held"].append(handle)
        write_state(handle, patch)
        log("%s: resume %s -- %s" % (handle, row["outcome"], detail))
    return result, 0


# ------------------------------------------------------------------ the modes
def cmd_law():
    readings = sentence_law()
    phrase = {"hook": RESUME_HOOK, "read": RESUME_TURN is not None,
              "why": "" if RESUME_TURN is not None else no_phrase_detail()}
    print(json.dumps({"sentences": readings, "lawful": lawful(), "resume_phrase": phrase},
                     indent=2))
    if RESUME_TURN is None:
        log("the resume phrase is not read: %s. The live and the gone resume roads refuse "
            "until the orchestrate skill is in the machine tier." % no_phrase_detail())
    for r in readings:
        if r["lawful"]:
            continue
        log("THE SENTENCE LAW IS BROKEN: %s interpolates %s, and the ruling allows nothing "
            "at all." % (r["name"], r["format_fields"] or [r["percent_fields"]]))
    return 0 if lawful() else 1


def cmd_set_workpaper(handle, path):
    """Record which workpaper a handle's stamp is read from.

    The search answers most handles. It cannot answer one whose recorded directory
    holds several projects, and the line in the human's queue names this command
    because a guess there would read the wrong project's clock and call the write
    done.
    """
    full = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(path))
    if not os.path.isfile(full):
        log("no file at %s, so nothing was recorded" % full)
        return 2
    write_state(handle, {"workpaper": full, "workpaper_set_at": now_iso()})
    print(json.dumps({"handle": handle, "workpaper": full,
                      "state": state_path(handle)}, indent=2))
    return 0


def cmd_clear_hold(handle):
    data = read_state(handle)
    if not data:
        log("no state for '%s' at %s" % (handle, state_path(handle)))
        return 0
    had = data.get("hold")
    write_state(handle, {"hold": None, "hold_cleared_at": now_iso()})
    print(json.dumps({"handle": handle, "cleared": bool(had),
                      "was": had, "state": state_path(handle)}, indent=2))
    return 0


def main(argv):
    args = list(argv)
    if not args or args[0] in ("-h", "--help"):
        sys.stderr.write(__doc__)
        return 2
    mode = args[0]
    if mode in ("--law", "--check", "--once"):
        load_resume_turn()
    if mode == "--law":
        return cmd_law() if len(args) == 1 else 2
    if mode == "--clear-hold":
        return cmd_clear_hold(args[1]) if len(args) == 2 else 2
    if mode == "--set-workpaper":
        return cmd_set_workpaper(args[1], args[2]) if len(args) == 3 else 2
    # The two numbers a proof moves. Each is read the same way, so a third one
    # later is one entry rather than one more block.
    numbers = {"--idle-stop-minutes": IDLE_STOP_MINUTES, "--activity-hours": ACTIVITY_HOURS}
    for flag in list(numbers):
        if flag not in args[1:]:
            continue
        i = args.index(flag)
        if len(args) <= i + 1 or not args[i + 1].isdigit():
            sys.stderr.write("%s takes a whole number\n" % flag)
            return 2
        numbers[flag] = int(args[i + 1])
        del args[i:i + 2]
    idle_stop = numbers["--idle-stop-minutes"]
    activity_hours = numbers["--activity-hours"]
    allow_tree = "--allow-source-tree" in args[1:]
    if allow_tree:
        args.remove("--allow-source-tree")
    only = set(args[1:])
    if mode not in ("--check", "--once") or (mode == "--check" and only) \
            or any(a.startswith("-") for a in only):
        sys.stderr.write("usage: session-continuity [--check | --once [<handle> ...]] "
                         "[--idle-stop-minutes <n>] [--activity-hours <n>] [--allow-source-tree] | --law | "
                         "--clear-hold <handle> | --set-workpaper <handle> <path>\n")
        return 2

    # The law is proved before anything is delivered, every run. A build that
    # broke it must not reach a session at all.
    if not lawful():
        log("refusing to run: a fixed sentence interpolates. See --law.")
        return 1

    me = pwd.getpwuid(os.geteuid()).pw_name
    bus_dir = conf_value("ORCHESTRATE_SHARED_BUS") or ""
    guard, guard_why = load_module(beside("session-guard"), "session-guard")
    rail, rail_why = load_module(beside("bus-nudge"), "bus-nudge")
    adapter_dir = beside("bus-nudge-adapters")
    cmd = harness()

    # EVERY WALL IS NAMED, AND A MISSING NUDGE IS ONE OF THEM. The twin check's
    # safe branch is a nudge. Without the rail beside it this program would find
    # a twin and have nowhere to send it, and the only move left would be the
    # resume the check just refused.
    blocking = []
    if not bus_dir:
        blocking.append("%s names no ORCHESTRATE_SHARED_BUS, so this machine declares no "
                        "bus and this program acts on none" % CONF)
    elif not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(bus_dir, "handles.json")):
        blocking.append("%s names %s as the bus and there is no board there"
                        % (CONF, bus_dir))
    if guard is None:
        blocking.append(guard_why + ". It says which processes carry a session, which is "
                        "half the twin check")
    if rail is None:
        blocking.append(rail_why + ". Its sentence is the one a twin is nudged with")
    elif not callable(getattr(rail, "nudge_text", None)):
        blocking.append("the wake rail at %s has no nudge_text()" % beside("bus-nudge"))
    if not os.access(os.path.join(adapter_dir, "claude"), os.X_OK):
        blocking.append("no wake-rail adapter at %s/claude, so a twin could not be nudged"
                        % adapter_dir)
    if not cmd:
        blocking.append("no 'claude' command for this account, so no session can be resumed")

    if mode == "--check":
        window, window_source = compaction_window()
        out = {"user": me, "conf": CONF, "bus_dir": bus_dir, "state_dir": state_dir(),
               "session_guard": beside("session-guard"), "wake_rail": beside("bus-nudge"),
               "adapter_dir": adapter_dir, "harness": cmd,
               "fallback_model": conf_value("ORCHESTRATE_DELEGATE_MODEL") or "",
               "sentences": sentence_law(), "alive_after_secs": ALIVE_AFTER_SECS,
               "resume_phrase": {"hook": RESUME_HOOK, "read": RESUME_TURN is not None,
                                 "why": "" if RESUME_TURN is not None else no_phrase_detail()},
               "idle_stop_minutes": idle_stop, "activity_hours": activity_hours,
               "stamp_grace_minutes": STAMP_GRACE_MINUTES,
               "compaction_records": records_path(), "record_ttl_hours": RECORD_TTL_HOURS,
               "window": window, "window_source": window_source,
               "token_threshold": token_threshold(window),
               "holds": [{"handle": h, "scope": hold_scope(v), "kind": v.get("kind") or "",
                          "retry_at": v.get("retry_at") if hold_scope(v) == "handle" else None,
                          "hold": v} for h, v in holds()],
               "ready": not blocking, "blocking": blocking}
        if not blocking:
            checked, _ = run_pass(guard, rail, adapter_dir, cmd, bus_dir, me,
                                  idle_stop_minutes=idle_stop,
                                  activity_hours=activity_hours, act=False)
            for key in ("rows", "stops", "compactions", "writes", "stamps"):
                out[key] = checked.get(key)
            if checked.get("blocking"):
                out["ready"], out["blocking"] = False, checked["blocking"]
        print(json.dumps(out, indent=2))
        return 0 if out["ready"] else 3

    if blocking:
        for line in blocking:
            log("refusing to run: %s" % line)
        return 3

    # ONLY THE INSTALLED COPY ACTS. A copy read out of a source tree finds no
    # siblings there, so beside() hands it the installed wake rail, adapters and
    # conf, and its acting pass would drive the claw's real board. Found in w300.
    # From anywhere else a pass prints what it would do and acts on nothing,
    # unless --allow-source-tree says the caller means it.
    if not installed_copy() and not allow_tree:
        log("not acting: this copy runs from %s, not %s, so it acts on nothing. It "
            "printed what a pass would do. Pass --allow-source-tree to act from here."
            % (os.path.realpath(__file__), INSTALLED))
        result, _ = run_pass(guard, rail, adapter_dir, cmd, bus_dir, me, only, idle_stop,
                             activity_hours, act=False)
        result["acting"] = False
        result["not_acting_because"] = "not the installed copy at %s" % INSTALLED
        # Under the journal the line above is the record, and the block is not.
        if not os.environ.get("JOURNAL_STREAM"):
            print(json.dumps(result, indent=2))
        return 5

    # BEFORE THE PASS READS THE STATE DIRECTORY, and only on a pass that acts.
    # --check returns above this line, so a reading changes nothing on disk.
    dropped = prune_state()
    if dropped:
        log("pruned state for %s: nothing has written to it in thirty days"
            % ", ".join(dropped))

    result, code = run_pass(guard, rail, adapter_dir, cmd, bus_dir, me, only, idle_stop,
                            activity_hours)
    result["pruned"] = dropped
    result["acting"] = True
    # UNDER THE JOURNAL, ONE LINE A PASS AND ONE LINE AN ACT. NEVER THE BLOCK.
    #
    # The sweeper beside this program writes nothing at all on a quiet pass,
    # because a result block every five minutes per account is the journal volume
    # the wake rail was fixed for. This rail starts sessions, and silence about
    # that is worth more than the bytes: a stopped instance and a quiet board
    # read the same, which is the failure wake-rail.md names about the rail
    # itself. So a pass says what it saw and what it decided in one short line,
    # and each act says what it did in one more, written where the act happened.
    #
    # THE BLOCK IS GONE FROM THE JOURNAL, WHICH IS FINDING 3 OF w292's HUB RIDE.
    # An acting pass printed its whole result, about four kilobytes, and on a busy
    # box a pass runs every two minutes. That is a few megabytes of journal a day
    # per account for a reading nobody takes from the journal. `--check` prints the
    # same structure whole, and a hand run of `--once` still does, so nothing that
    # read the block has lost it.
    #
    # The cost of what is left is stated rather than assumed: one line every two
    # minutes is about seven hundred a day per account, against the forty-three
    # thousand a day the loop this rail sits beside was fixed for.
    if os.environ.get("JOURNAL_STREAM"):
        said = ["%s %s" % (r["handle"], r.get("outcome") or r["action"])
                for r in result["rows"] if r["action"] != "idle"]
        said += ["%s %s" % (t["handle"], t["outcome"]) for t in result["stops"]]
        said += ["%s compaction %s" % (c.get("handle") or c.get("session"), c["outcome"])
                 for c in result["compactions"] if c["outcome"] != "not ours"]
        said += ["%s write %s" % (w["handle"], w["outcome"])
                 for w in result["writes"] if w["outcome"] not in ("idle", "skipped")]
        said += ["%s %s stamp %s" % (s["handle"], s["turn"], s["outcome"])
                 for s in result["stamps"] if s["outcome"] != "waiting"]
        log("%d orchestrator handle(s), %d with mail, %s" % (
            len(result["rows"]),
            len([r for r in result["rows"] if r["unread"] > 0]),
            ", ".join(said) or "nothing to do"))
    else:
        print(json.dumps(result, indent=2))
    return code


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
